window.PYQ_DATA.add({"id":"px-ncert12-chem-electrochemistry","kind":"practice","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"title":"Electrochemistry · Practice","chapter":"Electrochemistry","subject":"Chemistry","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","marking":{"mcq":[4,-1]},"rules":{},"note":"","questions":[{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-001","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":1,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using the standard electrode potentials (V): $Zn^{2+}/Zn$ = −0.76, $Fe^{2+}/Fe$ = −0.44, $Ni^{2+}/Ni$ = −0.25, $H^+/H_2$ = 0.00, $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ = 0.34, $I_2/I^-$ = 0.54, $Ag^+/Ag$ = 0.80, $Br_2/Br^-$ = 1.09, $Cl_2/Cl^-$ = 1.36, $Au^{3+}/Au$ = 1.40.\nHow many of the following reactions are feasible under standard conditions?\n(a) $Zn + Fe^{2+} \\rightarrow Zn^{2+} + Fe$\n(b) $2Ag + Cu^{2+} \\rightarrow 2Ag^+ + Cu$\n(c) $Cl_2 + 2Br^- \\rightarrow 2Cl^- + Br_2$\n(d) $I_2 + 2Br^- \\rightarrow 2I^- + Br_2$\n(e) $Ni + 2H^+ \\rightarrow Ni^{2+} + H_2$\n(f) $2Au + 6H^+ \\rightarrow 2Au^{3+} + 3H_2$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A reaction is feasible if $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ$(species reduced) − $E^\\circ$(species oxidised) > 0.\n(a) −0.44 − (−0.76) = +0.32 V: feasible.\n(b) 0.34 − 0.80 < 0: not feasible.\n(c) 1.36 − 1.09 > 0: feasible.\n(d) 0.54 − 1.09 < 0: not feasible.\n(e) 0 − (−0.25) > 0: feasible.\n(f) 0 − 1.40 < 0: not feasible.\nSo 3 reactions are feasible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-002","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":2,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The conductivity of a 0.02 mol $L^{-1}$ acetic acid solution at 298 K is $2.3 \\times 10^{-4}$ S $cm^{-1}$. Given $\\lambda^\\circ(H^+) = 349.6$ S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(CH_3COO^-) = 40.9$ S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$, what is the degree of dissociation of acetic acid at this concentration?","image":"","options":["0.0115","0.0589","0.0294","0.294"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa = 2.3\\times10^{-4}$ S $cm^{-1}$, c = 0.02 mol $L^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{c}$ and $\\alpha = \\frac{\\Lambda_m}{\\Lambda_m^\\circ}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{2.3\\times10^{-4}\\times1000}{0.02} = 11.5$ S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$.\nBy Kohlrausch law, $\\Lambda_m^\\circ = 349.6 + 40.9 = 390.5$ S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$.\nSo $\\alpha = 11.5/390.5 = 0.0294$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-003","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":3,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a metallic wire and an aqueous electrolyte solution are both heated, the conductance of the wire falls while that of the solution rises. Also, a prolonged direct current changes the composition of the solution but not of the wire.\nStatement II: Superconductors have infinite resistivity, and earlier they were known only among metals and alloys at 0 to 15 K.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct, in both of its parts:\n- Electronic conductance decreases with temperature, while ionic conductance increases with temperature.\n- In a metal, electrons enter at one end and leave at the other, so its composition is unchanged. Prolonged DC through an ionic solution causes electrochemical reactions that change its composition.\nStatement II is incorrect. By definition, superconductors have zero resistivity (infinite conductivity), not infinite resistivity. The second half, about metals and alloys at 0 to 15 K, is true.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-004","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":4,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (electrolysis using inert Pt electrodes) with List-II (main product at the stated electrode).\nList-I:\n(A) Molten NaCl – cathode\n(B) Aqueous NaCl – cathode\n(C) Dilute $H_2SO_4$ – anode\n(D) Concentrated $H_2SO_4$ – anode\nList-II:\n(I) $H_2$ gas\n(II) Sodium metal\n(III) $S_2O_8^{2-}$ ions\n(IV) $O_2$ gas","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Molten NaCl contains only $Na^+$ as the cation, so Na metal forms at the cathode: A-II.\n(B) In aqueous NaCl, $H^+/H_2O$ reduction ($E^\\circ$ = 0.00 V) is preferred over $Na^+$ ($-2.71$ V), so $H_2$ forms: B-I.\n(C) In dilute $H_2SO_4$, water is oxidised to $O_2$: C-IV.\n(D) In concentrated $H_2SO_4$, $2SO_4^{2-} \\rightarrow S_2O_8^{2-} + 2e^-$ is preferred: D-III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-005","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":5,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the cell representation $Zn(s)|Zn^{2+}(aq)||Cu^{2+}(aq)|Cu(s)$, what does the double vertical line $||$ stand for?","image":"","options":["The boundary between a metal and its own electrolyte","The salt bridge connecting the two electrolyte solutions","The external metallic wire carrying electrons through the voltmeter","An inert platinum electrode placed between the solutions"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By convention, a single vertical line separates a metal from its electrolyte solution. This is a phase boundary.\nA double vertical line is placed between the two electrolytes that are joined by a salt bridge.\nThe external wire is not shown in cell notation at all.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-006","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":6,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During electrolysis of aqueous NaCl with inert electrodes, Cl$_2$ rather than O$_2$ is liberated at the anode. This happens even though the oxidation of water has the lower $E^\\circ$ value (1.23 V vs 1.36 V), and it is due to the overpotential of oxygen.\nStatement II: During electrolysis of dilute sulphuric acid with inert electrodes, peroxydisulphate (S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$) ions are the main product at the anode.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Water oxidation (1.23 V) should be preferred over Cl$^-$ oxidation (1.36 V). However, the overpotential of oxygen makes Cl$^-$ oxidation the actual anode reaction, so Cl$_2$ forms.\nStatement II is incorrect. In dilute H$_2$SO$_4$, water is oxidised to O$_2$ (1.23 V). The formation of S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$ (1.96 V) is preferred only at higher acid concentrations.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-007","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":7,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell used in the Apollo space programme, which substances are incorporated into the porous carbon electrodes to increase the rate of the electrode reactions?","image":"","options":["Manganese dioxide and graphite powder","Finely divided platinum or palladium","Lead dioxide","Zinc–mercury amalgam"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In this cell, H$_2$ and O$_2$ are bubbled through porous carbon electrodes into concentrated aqueous NaOH.\nNCERT states that catalysts such as finely divided Pt or Pd are built into these electrodes to speed up the electrode reactions.\nMnO$_2$ belongs to the dry cell, PbO$_2$ to the lead storage battery, and Zn–Hg amalgam to the mercury cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-008","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":8,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The Daniell cell reaction $\\text{Zn(s)} + \\text{Cu}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Zn}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + \\text{Cu(s)}$ is rewritten as $2\\text{Zn(s)} + 2\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow 2\\text{Zn}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + 2\\text{Cu(s)}$. How do $E^\\circ_{cell}$ and $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ change?","image":"","options":["Both $E^\\circ_{cell}$ and $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ are doubled","$E^\\circ_{cell}$ is doubled while $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ is unchanged","$E^\\circ_{cell}$ is unchanged while $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ is doubled","Both remain unchanged"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"$E_{cell}$ is an intensive property. It does not depend on the amount of substance reacting, so $E^\\circ_{cell}$ stays at 1.1 V.\n$\\Delta_rG$ is an extensive property. In $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$, n rises from 2 to 4.\nSo $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ changes from $-2FE^\\circ$ to $-4FE^\\circ$, i.e., it doubles.\nOption (1) wrongly treats the potential as extensive.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-009","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":9,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Copper metal does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid under standard conditions.\nReason (R): The standard electrode potential of the Cu$^{2+}$/Cu couple is positive (+0.34 V), so H$^+$ ions cannot oxidise copper.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: Cu does not dissolve in HCl.\nR is true: $E^\\circ$(Cu$^{2+}$/Cu) = +0.34 V. This means Cu$^{2+}$ is reduced more easily than H$^+$.\nSo the reverse process, H$^+$ oxidising Cu, cannot occur under standard conditions.\nR therefore correctly explains A. In nitric acid, copper is oxidised by nitrate ion, not by H$^+$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-010","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":10,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A conductivity cell filled with 0.1 mol L$^{-1}$ KCl solution (conductivity 1.29 S m$^{-1}$) shows a resistance of 50 $\\Omega$. The same cell filled with 0.05 mol L$^{-1}$ NaCl solution shows a resistance of 120 $\\Omega$. The molar conductivity of the NaCl solution is:","image":"","options":["107.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","53.75 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","215.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","1.075 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$(KCl) = 1.29 S m$^{-1}$, R(KCl) = 50 $\\Omega$, R(NaCl) = 120 $\\Omega$, c(NaCl) = 0.05 M = 50 mol m$^{-3}$.\nCell constant: $G^* = \\kappa R = 1.29 \\times 50 = 64.5$ m$^{-1}$.\nConductivity of NaCl: $\\kappa = G^*/R = 64.5/120 = 0.5375$ S m$^{-1}$.\nMolar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa/c = 0.5375/50 = 1.075 \\times 10^{-2}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nConverting units: $1.075 \\times 10^{-2} \\times 10^4 = 107.5$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-011","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":11,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The potential difference between the two electrodes of a galvanic cell is called its emf when no current is drawn from the cell.\nStatement II: By the accepted convention, the anode is written on the right-hand side when a galvanic cell is represented.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The cell potential is called the emf when no current is drawn through the cell.\nStatement II is incorrect. The convention keeps the anode on the left and the cathode on the right, which gives $E_{cell} = E_{right} - E_{left}$ as a positive value.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-012","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":12,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the cell Ni(s) | Ni$^{2+}$(0.1 M) || Ag$^+$(0.01 M) | Ag(s) at 298 K, find $\\Delta_r G$ for the reaction Ni(s) + 2Ag$^+$(aq) → Ni$^{2+}$(aq) + 2Ag(s), as written.\nGiven: $E^\\circ$(Ag$^+$/Ag) = 0.80 V, $E^\\circ$(Ni$^{2+}$/Ni) = $-$0.25 V, $\\frac{2.303RT}{F}$ = 0.059 V, F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["$-185.6$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-92.8$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-202.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-219.7$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0.80 - (-0.25) = 1.05$ V and n = 2.\nFormula: $E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$ and $\\Delta_r G = -nFE_{cell}$.\nSubstituting: $Q = 0.1/(0.01)^2 = 10^3$, so $E_{cell} = 1.05 - 0.0295 \\times 3 = 0.9615$ V.\n$\\Delta_r G = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 0.9615 = -185570$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta_r G$ = $-185.6$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. Option (3) wrongly uses $E^\\circ$; option (4) adds the Nernst term instead of subtracting it.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-013","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":13,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following are 1-1 type electrolytes?\nNaCl, $CaCl_2$, KBr, $MgSO_4$, $KNO_3$, $Na_2SO_4$, HCl","image":"","options":["5","4","3","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"An electrolyte's type is set by the charges on its cation and anion.\n1-1 type: NaCl, KBr, $KNO_3$ and HCl, each giving a singly charged cation and anion. That makes 4.\n$CaCl_2$ is 2-1 type.\n$MgSO_4$ is 2-2 type.\n$Na_2SO_4$ is 1-2 type, since it gives $Na^+$ and $SO_4^{2-}$.\nThe count is 4.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-014","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":14,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The conductivity of a 0.1 mol L$^{-1}$ KCl solution at 298 K is 0.0129 S cm$^{-1}$. What is its value in S m$^{-1}$?","image":"","options":["$1.29 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^{-1}$","0.129 S m$^{-1}$","1.29 S m$^{-1}$","129 S m$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$ = 0.0129 S cm$^{-1}$.\nConversion: 1 S cm$^{-1}$ = 100 S m$^{-1}$. A cm$^{-1}$ in the denominator becomes $(0.01\\ \\text{m})^{-1}$ = 100 m$^{-1}$.\nSubstituting: 0.0129 × 100 = 1.29 S m$^{-1}$.\nThis matches the value listed for 0.1 M KCl in Table 2.3.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-015","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":15,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For dilute aqueous solutions of KCl at 298 K, molar conductivity $\\Lambda_m$ is plotted on the y-axis against $c^{1/2}$ on the x-axis. Which description of the graph is correct?","image":"","options":["A straight line rising with positive slope, whose intercept on the y-axis is zero","A curve that rises steeply near $c^{1/2} = 0$, so it cannot be extrapolated","A straight line with negative slope equal to $-A$ and y-intercept equal to $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$","A straight line with slope equal to $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ and y-intercept equal to $-A$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"KCl is a strong electrolyte. Its molar conductivity obeys $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - A c^{1/2}$.\nThis has the form y = c + mx, so the plot is a straight line with slope $-A$ (falling as c increases).\nThe y-intercept, at $c^{1/2} = 0$, gives $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$.\nOption (b) describes a weak electrolyte such as acetic acid, not KCl.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-016","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":16,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Three galvanic cells are studied at 298 K, with all ions at 1 M and gases at 1 bar.\nCell I: $Pt|H_2(g)|H^+(aq)||Cu^{2+}(aq)|Cu$ shows an emf of 0.34 V.\nCell II: $Zn|Zn^{2+}(aq)||Cu^{2+}(aq)|Cu$ shows an emf of 1.10 V.\nCell III is made of a copper half-cell and a silver half-cell. Its voltmeter reads 0.46 V, and silver is the positive terminal.\nWhat is the standard emf of the cell $Zn|Zn^{2+}(aq)||Ag^+(aq)|Ag$?","image":"","options":["0.64 V","0.80 V","1.56 V","2.36 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: SHE is the anode in Cell I, so $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ = 0.34 V.\nCell II: 1.10 = 0.34 − $E^\\circ(Zn^{2+}/Zn)$, so $E^\\circ(Zn^{2+}/Zn)$ = −0.76 V.\nCell III: Ag is the cathode, so 0.46 = $E^\\circ(Ag^+/Ag)$ − 0.34, giving $E^\\circ(Ag^+/Ag)$ = 0.80 V.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_R - E^\\circ_L$ = 0.80 − (−0.76).\nSo $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 1.56 V.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-017","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":17,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A galvanic cell has a positive standard cell potential ($E^\\circ_{cell} > 0$) at 298 K. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ of the cell reaction is negative.\n(ii) The equilibrium constant of the cell reaction is greater than 1.\n(iii) The cell reaction is spontaneous in the forward direction under standard conditions.\n(iv) $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ has the same value however the balanced equation is multiplied.\n(v) $E^\\circ_{cell}$ doubles when all stoichiometric coefficients are doubled.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ$, so a positive $E^\\circ$ gives a negative $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$.\n(ii) Correct: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -RT\\ln K < 0$ means $\\ln K > 0$, so K > 1.\n(iii) Correct: a negative $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ means the forward reaction is spontaneous.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ is extensive and scales with n.\n(v) Incorrect: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ is intensive and does not change.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-018","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":18,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the relation $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - A c^{1/2}$ for strong electrolytes in water at a fixed temperature.\nStatement I: KCl and $NaNO_3$ have the same value of the constant A.\nStatement II: $CaCl_2$ and $MgSO_4$ have the same value of A, because both are salts of divalent cations.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Statement I is false but Statement II is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"For a given solvent and temperature, A depends on the electrolyte type, that is, the charges on its ions.\nStatement I is true. KCl and $NaNO_3$ are both 1-1 electrolytes, so they share the same A.\nStatement II is false. $CaCl_2$ is a 2-1 electrolyte, while $MgSO_4$ is 2-2, so their A values differ.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-019","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":19,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the Daniell cell reaction $Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \\to Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)$.\nStatement I: If the reaction is written as $2Zn + 2Cu^{2+} \\to 2Zn^{2+} + 2Cu$, the value of $\\Delta_r G$ doubles.\nStatement II: Writing the reaction with doubled coefficients also doubles $E_{cell}$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. $\\Delta_r G = -nFE_{cell}$ and $\\Delta_r G$ is extensive. With doubled coefficients, n changes from 2 to 4, so $\\Delta_r G$ becomes $-4FE_{cell}$, which is double.\nStatement II is incorrect. $E_{cell}$ is an intensive property and does not depend on how the equation is multiplied.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-020","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":20,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"An electrolyte solution has a resistivity of 0.25 $\\Omega$ m. It fills a cell whose electrodes each have an area of 2.0 cm$^2$ and are 0.50 cm apart. What is the conductance of the solution between the electrodes?","image":"","options":["0.16 S","16 S","1.6 $\\times 10^{-3}$ S","1.6 S"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\rho$ = 0.25 $\\Omega$ m, so $\\kappa = \\frac{1}{\\rho}$ = 4.0 S m$^{-1}$.\nGeometry: $\\frac{l}{A} = \\frac{0.50\\ \\text{cm}}{2.0\\ \\text{cm}^2}$ = 0.25 cm$^{-1}$ = 25 m$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $G = \\kappa \\frac{A}{l}$.\nSubstituting: $G = \\frac{4.0}{25}$ = 0.16 S, which means R = 6.25 $\\Omega$.\nOption 2 (16 S) results from forgetting to convert cm$^{-1}$ to m$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-021","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":21,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using the standard electrode potentials (V): $Cl_2/Cl^-$ = 1.36, $Br_2/Br^-$ = 1.09, $I_2/I^-$ = 0.54, $Fe^{2+}/Fe$ = −0.44, $Ni^{2+}/Ni$ = −0.25, $Al^{3+}/Al$ = −1.66, $Cr^{3+}/Cr$ = −0.74, $H_2O_2,H^+/H_2O$ = 1.78, $Fe^{3+}/Fe^{2+}$ = 0.77.\nHow many of the following reactions are feasible under standard conditions?\n(i) $Cl_2$ + 2$Br^-$ → 2$Cl^-$ + $Br_2$\n(ii) $I_2$ + 2$Cl^-$ → 2$I^-$ + $Cl_2$\n(iii) Ni + $Fe^{2+}$ → $Ni^{2+}$ + Fe\n(iv) Al + $Cr^{3+}$ → $Al^{3+}$ + Cr\n(v) $H_2O_2$ + 2$H^+$ + 2$Fe^{2+}$ → 2$H_2O$ + 2$Fe^{3+}$","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A reaction is feasible if $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ$(species reduced) − $E^\\circ$(species oxidised) > 0.\n(i) 1.36 − 1.09 = +0.27 V: feasible.\n(ii) 0.54 − 1.36 < 0: not feasible.\n(iii) −0.44 − (−0.25) < 0: not feasible.\n(iv) −0.74 − (−1.66) = +0.92 V: feasible.\n(v) 1.78 − 0.77 = +1.01 V: feasible.\nThree reactions are feasible.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-022","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":22,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"During rusting of iron, oxygen is reduced at the cathodic spot in the presence of $H^+$ ions. According to NCERT, these $H^+$ ions are believed to come mainly from:","image":"","options":["Hydrolysis of $Fe^{3+}$ ions already present in rust","Dissolution of nitrogen gas of air in water","Carbonic acid formed when atmospheric $CO_2$ dissolves in water","Decomposition of $Fe_2O_3 \\cdot xH_2O$ on the surface"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT states that $H^+$ is believed to be available from $H_2CO_3$. This acid forms when $CO_2$ from air dissolves in the water film on the metal.\nOther acidic oxides from the atmosphere can also supply $H^+$.\n$N_2$ does not form an acid in water.\nRust is a product of corrosion, not the source of $H^+$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-023","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":23,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A copper rod dips in $Cu(NO_3)_2$ solution and a silver rod dips in $AgNO_3$ solution. The two beakers are joined by a salt bridge, and the rods are connected by a wire through a voltmeter. The cell reaction is $Cu(s) + 2Ag^+(aq) \\rightarrow Cu^{2+}(aq) + 2Ag(s)$. Which statement about this set-up is correct?","image":"","options":["Electrons flow through the wire from the silver rod to the copper rod","The silver rod is the anode and slowly dissolves","Electrons flow through the wire from the copper rod to the silver rod","Conventional current flows through the wire from the copper rod to the silver rod"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Copper is oxidised ($Cu \\rightarrow Cu^{2+} + 2e^-$), so copper is the anode (negative electrode).\nSilver ions are reduced at the silver rod, so silver is the cathode (positive electrode).\nElectrons travel through the wire from the negative copper rod to the positive silver rod.\nConventional current flows in the opposite direction, from Ag to Cu, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-024","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":24,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $\\Lambda_m$ is the conductance of the volume of solution containing 1 mol of electrolyte, kept between electrodes at unit distance apart.\n(ii) On dilution, the decrease in $\\kappa$ is more than compensated by the increase in volume V, so $\\Lambda_m$ increases.\n(iii) For acetic acid, $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is the intercept of the $\\Lambda_m$ versus $c^{1/2}$ plot.\n(iv) At infinite dilution, a weak electrolyte dissociates completely ($\\alpha = 1$).\n(v) The constant A in $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - Ac^{1/2}$ depends on the solvent and temperature, as well as on the electrolyte type.\n(vi) The conductivity of acetic acid solution increases on dilution because $\\alpha$ increases.","image":"","options":["3","5","4","2"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: this is the definition, with l = 1 and A = V, giving $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa V$.\n(ii) Correct: this is why $\\Lambda_m$ rises on dilution.\n(iii) Incorrect: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ of a weak electrolyte cannot be found by extrapolation. Kohlrausch law is used instead.\n(iv) Correct: as c approaches zero, $\\alpha$ approaches 1.\n(v) Correct: A is fixed for a given solvent, temperature and electrolyte type.\n(vi) Incorrect: $\\kappa$ always decreases on dilution, even for weak electrolytes.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-025","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":25,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the standard emf of the cell $Cu(s)|Cu^{2+}(aq, 1 M)||Ag^+(aq, 1 M)|Ag(s)$.\nGiven: $E^\\circ_{Ag^+/Ag} = 0.80$ V and $E^\\circ_{Cu^{2+}/Cu} = 0.34$ V.","image":"","options":["1.14 V","0.46 V","−0.46 V","1.26 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the cathode (right) is Ag with $E^\\circ$ = 0.80 V, and the anode (left) is Cu with $E^\\circ$ = 0.34 V.\nFormula: $E_{cell} = E_{right} - E_{left}$.\nSubstituting: $E_{cell}$ = 0.80 − 0.34.\nSo $E_{cell}$ = 0.46 V. The potentials are not doubled even though 2 Ag$^+$ ions react, because electrode potential is an intensive quantity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-026","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":26,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the electrochemical picture of rusting, which reaction takes place at the spot on the iron object that acts as the anode?","image":"","options":["$O_2(g) + 4H^+(aq) + 4e^- \\rightarrow 2H_2O(l)$","$Fe(s) \\rightarrow Fe^{2+}(aq) + 2e^-$","$Fe^{3+}(aq) + e^- \\rightarrow Fe^{2+}(aq)$","$2H^+(aq) + 2e^- \\rightarrow H_2(g)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The anode is the spot where oxidation occurs.\nAt this anodic spot, iron loses electrons: $Fe \\rightarrow Fe^{2+} + 2e^-$, with $E^\\circ(Fe^{2+}/Fe) = -0.44$ V.\nOption (1) is a reduction. It occurs at the cathodic spot, so it is the most tempting wrong choice.\nOptions (3) and (4) are also reductions and are not part of the rusting scheme.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-027","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":27,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the half-cell Pt(s)|$Br_2$(aq)|$Br^-$(aq), the platinum metal:","image":"","options":["gets oxidised to $Pt^{2+}$ and supplies electrons to bromine","takes no part in the reaction but provides a surface for the redox change and conducts electrons","is reduced by bromide ions during the cell reaction","acts as a catalyst that raises the standard electrode potential of the couple"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Pt and Au are used as inert electrodes.\nThey do not take part in the half-cell reaction ½$Br_2$ + $e^-$ → $Br^-$.\nThey only provide a surface for oxidation or reduction and carry electrons to and from the external circuit.\nOption (4) fails because Pt does not change $E^\\circ$; the potential depends only on the $Br_2/Br^-$ couple.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-028","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":28,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A cylindrical column of 0.025 mol L$^{-1}$ KOH solution has a diameter of 2 cm and a length of 40 cm. Its electrical resistance is $2.0 \\times 10^{3}$ $\\Omega$. Taking $\\pi$ = 3.14, what is the molar conductivity of the solution?","image":"","options":["63.7 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","254.8 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","1019 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","0.255 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: radius = 1 cm, so A = 3.14 × $1^2$ = 3.14 cm$^2$; l = 40 cm; R = 2000 $\\Omega$.\nResistivity: $\\rho = \\frac{RA}{l}$ = 2000 × 3.14/40 = 157 $\\Omega$ cm.\nConductivity: $\\kappa = 1/\\rho$ = 6.369 × $10^{-3}$ S cm$^{-1}$.\nMolar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{M}$ = 6.369 × $10^{-3}$ × 1000/0.025 = 254.8 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nUsing the diameter as the radius gives 63.7; omitting the factor 1000 gives 0.255.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-029","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":29,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The cell constant of a conductivity cell is usually found by measuring its resistance when filled with a KCl solution of accurately known conductivity.\nReason (R): Directly measuring the distance between the electrodes and their area of cross-section is inconvenient and unreliable.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: the cell constant is obtained from $G^* = R\\kappa$, using KCl solutions whose conductivity is accurately known (Table 2.3).\nR is true: NCERT states that measuring l and A is not only inconvenient but also unreliable.\nR is the very reason the KCl calibration method is used, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-030","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":30,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the overall rusting reaction $2Fe(s) + O_2(g) + 4H^+(aq) \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+}(aq) + 2H_2O(l)$, calculate $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ at 298 K.\nGiven: $E^\\circ(Fe^{2+}/Fe) = -0.44$ V, $E^\\circ(O_2, H^+/H_2O) = 1.23$ V, $F = 96500$ C mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["$-644.6$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-322.3$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-304.9$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+644.6$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: cathode $E^\\circ = 1.23$ V, anode $E^\\circ = -0.44$ V, and $n = 4$ (4 electrons reduce one $O_2$).\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$ and $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$.\nSubstituting: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.23 - (-0.44) = 1.67$ V.\n$\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -4 \\times 96500 \\times 1.67 = -644620$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -644.6$ kJ mol$^{-1}$. The negative sign shows that rusting is spontaneous.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-031","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":31,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A conductivity cell is filled with 0.1 mol L$^{-1}$ KCl solution, whose conductivity is 1.29 S m$^{-1}$ at 298 K. The measured resistance is 50 $\\Omega$. What is the cell constant?","image":"","options":["0.0258 m$^{-1}$","38.8 m$^{-1}$","64.5 m$^{-1}$","645 m$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$ = 1.29 S m$^{-1}$, R = 50 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $G^* = \\frac{l}{A} = R\\kappa$.\nSubstituting: $G^*$ = 50 × 1.29 = 64.5 m$^{-1}$.\nSo the cell constant = 64.5 m$^{-1}$, which has the dimension of length$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-032","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":32,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell) with List-II (feature):\nList-I\n(A) Leclanche (dry) cell\n(B) Mercury cell\n(C) Lead storage battery\n(D) $H_2$–$O_2$ fuel cell\nList-II\n(I) Electrolyte is about 38% sulphuric acid\n(II) Potential stays nearly constant (~1.35 V) because no ion in solution changes concentration\n(III) Used to provide electrical power in the Apollo space programme\n(IV) Manganese is reduced from +4 to +3 at the cathode","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Dry cell: the cathode reaction is $MnO_2 + NH_4^+ + e^- \\to MnO(OH) + NH_3$, so Mn goes from +4 to +3. This is IV.\n(B) Mercury cell: the overall reaction involves no ion in solution, so the voltage stays near 1.35 V. This is II.\n(C) Lead storage battery uses 38% $H_2SO_4$. This is I.\n(D) $H_2$–$O_2$ fuel cell powered the Apollo missions. This is III.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-033","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":33,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): If the Daniell cell reaction is written as $2Zn(s) + 2Cu^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow 2Zn^{2+}(aq) + 2Cu(s)$ instead of $Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)$, the value of $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ doubles, but $E^\\circ_{cell}$ stays 1.1 V.\nReason (R): $E_{cell}$ is an intensive property, while $\\Delta_r G$ is an extensive property whose value depends on $n$ in $\\Delta_r G = -nFE_{cell}$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Doubling the equation makes $n = 4$ instead of 2. So $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -4FE^\\circ$ instead of $-2FE^\\circ$, which is double the value. $E^\\circ_{cell}$ is still 1.1 V.\nR is true. Cell potential does not depend on the amount of reaction, so it is intensive. $\\Delta_r G$ scales with $n$, so it is extensive.\nR directly explains why one quantity doubles and the other does not.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-034","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":34,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How much charge is required to deposit 0.5 mol of aluminium from molten Al$_2$O$_3$ dissolved in cryolite? (F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["48250 C","144750 C","96500 C","289500 C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: n(Al) = 0.5 mol. The electrode reaction is Al$^{3+}$ + 3e$^-$ → Al.\nFormula: Q = (moles of metal) × (electrons per ion) × F\nSubstituting: Q = 0.5 × 3 × 96500 C\nSo Q = 144750 C\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-035","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":35,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A cell works on the reaction $2Fe^{3+}(aq) + 2I^-(aq) \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+}(aq) + I_2(s)$, with $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0.236$ V at 298 K. What is the equilibrium constant $K_c$ of this reaction? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V at 298 K.)","image":"","options":["$1 \\times 10^{8}$","$1 \\times 10^{4}$","$1 \\times 10^{16}$","$1 \\times 10^{-8}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0.236$ V. Two $Fe^{3+}$ ions each gain one electron, so $n = 2$.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log K_c$, which rearranges to $\\log K_c = \\frac{nE^\\circ}{0.059}$.\nSubstituting: $\\log K_c = \\frac{2 \\times 0.236}{0.059} = 8.0$.\nSo $K_c = 1 \\times 10^{8}$.\nTaking $n = 1$ would wrongly give $10^{4}$. The value $10^{-8}$ would be $K$ for the reverse reaction.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-036","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":36,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (electrolysis set-up) with List-II (products at cathode / anode).\nList-I:\n(A) Aqueous $AgNO_3$, silver electrodes\n(B) Aqueous $AgNO_3$, platinum electrodes\n(C) Dilute $H_2SO_4$, platinum electrodes\n(D) Concentrated $H_2SO_4$, platinum electrodes\nList-II:\n(I) $H_2$ / $O_2$\n(II) Ag deposited / Ag dissolves as $Ag^+$\n(III) $H_2$ / $S_2O_8^{2-}$\n(IV) Ag deposited / $O_2$","image":"","options":["A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Silver is a reactive electrode, so Ag deposits at the cathode and the Ag anode dissolves: II.\n(B) Pt is inert, so Ag deposits at the cathode and water is oxidised to $O_2$ at the anode: IV.\n(C) In dilute $H_2SO_4$, water oxidation (1.23 V) is preferred at the anode, with $H_2$ at the cathode: I.\n(D) At higher acid concentration, $2SO_4^{2-} \\rightarrow S_2O_8^{2-}$ is preferred at the anode: III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-037","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":37,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The density of the electrolyte in a lead storage battery decreases as the battery discharges.\nReason (R): In the overall discharge reaction, sulphuric acid is consumed and water is formed.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The discharge reaction is $Pb + PbO_2 + 2H_2SO_4 \\rightarrow 2PbSO_4 + 2H_2O$.\nR is true: H$_2$SO$_4$ is used up and water is produced.\nA is true: the acid becomes more dilute as a result, so its density falls.\nR therefore correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-038","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":38,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A hydrogen electrode, $Pt(s)|H_2(g, 1\\ bar)|H^+(aq)$, dips in a solution of pH 3 at 298 K. What is its electrode potential? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.)","image":"","options":["$-0.059$ V","$+0.177$ V","$-0.177$ V","$-0.354$ V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $p_{H_2} = 1$ bar and pH = 3, so $[H^+] = 10^{-3}$ M.\nFormula: for $H^+ + e^- \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}H_2$, $E = 0 - 0.059\\log\\frac{1}{[H^+]} = -0.059\\times pH$.\nSubstituting: $E = -0.059 \\times 3$.\nSo E = $-0.177$ V.\nThe potential becomes more negative as pH increases. The value $+0.177$ V has the wrong sign.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-039","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":39,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The standard emf of a Daniell cell, $Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)$, is 1.10 V at 298 K. What is the standard Gibbs energy of this reaction? (Take $F = 96500$ C $mol^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$-212.3$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$-106.2$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$-424.6$ kJ $mol^{-1}$","$+212.3$ kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.10$ V. Two electrons are transferred, so $n = 2$. $F = 96500$ C $mol^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 1.10 = -212300$ J $mol^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -212.3$ kJ $mol^{-1}$. The negative sign shows the reaction is spontaneous.\nTaking $n = 1$ would wrongly give $-106.2$ kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-040","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":40,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the electrolytic refining of copper using aqueous copper sulphate as the electrolyte, which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Pure copper is made the anode, and impurities deposit on the cathode","Both electrodes are platinum, and copper deposits from the solution onto the cathode","Impure copper is made the anode and dissolves, and pure copper is deposited on the cathode","Impure copper is made the cathode, and Cu$^{2+}$ ions are oxidised at it"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Key fact: in a CuSO$_4$ cell with copper electrodes, Cu is oxidised at the anode, Cu → Cu$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$.\nAt the cathode, Cu$^{2+}$ is reduced, Cu$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ → Cu.\nSo the impure copper is made the anode, where it dissolves, and pure copper collects on the cathode.\nOption (4) fails because reduction, not oxidation, occurs at the cathode. Option (2) fails because inert electrodes cannot purify an impure copper block.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-041","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":41,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell) with List-II (electrolyte used):\nList-I\n(a) Leclanché cell\n(b) Mercury cell\n(c) Lead storage battery\n(d) H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell (Apollo)\nList-II\n(i) About 38% solution of H$_2$SO$_4$\n(ii) Moist paste of NH$_4$Cl and ZnCl$_2$\n(iii) Concentrated aqueous NaOH\n(iv) Paste of KOH and ZnO","image":"","options":["a-(ii), b-(iv), c-(i), d-(iii)","a-(iv), b-(ii), c-(i), d-(iii)","a-(ii), b-(iii), c-(i), d-(iv)","a-(i), b-(iv), c-(ii), d-(iii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Dry cell: a moist paste of NH$_4$Cl and ZnCl$_2$ fills the space between the electrodes, so (ii).\n(b) Mercury cell: the electrolyte is a paste of KOH and ZnO, so (iv).\n(c) Lead storage battery: the electrolyte is a 38% H$_2$SO$_4$ solution, so (i).\n(d) Apollo fuel cell: the gases are bubbled into concentrated aqueous NaOH, so (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-042","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":42,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using Kohlrausch law, calculate the limiting molar conductivity of $K_2SO_4$ in water at 298 K. Given: $\\lambda^\\circ(K^+) = 73.5$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(SO_4^{2-}) = 160.0$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["233.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","307.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","393.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","467.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda^\\circ(K^+) = 73.5$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(SO_4^{2-}) = 160.0$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = \\nu_+\\lambda^\\circ_+ + \\nu_-\\lambda^\\circ_-$. Each formula unit of $K_2SO_4$ gives 2 $K^+$ and 1 $SO_4^{2-}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = 2(73.5) + 160.0$.\nSo $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ = 307.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (a) forgets the factor 2 for $K^+$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-043","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":43,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the origin of electrode potential are correct?\n(i) Metal ions from the solution tend to deposit on the electrode, trying to make it positively charged.\n(ii) Metal atoms entering the solution as ions leave electrons behind, trying to make the electrode negatively charged.\n(iii) At equilibrium, the electrode is always negatively charged with respect to the solution.\n(iv) The potential difference that develops between the electrode and the electrolyte is called the electrode potential.\n(v) The absolute potential of a single half-cell can be measured directly with a voltmeter.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: deposition of cations makes the electrode positive.\n(ii) Correct: atoms leaving as ions leave their electrons on the electrode, making it negative.\n(iii) Incorrect: depending on which tendency wins, the electrode may be either positive or negative.\n(iv) Correct: this is the definition of electrode potential.\n(v) Incorrect: only the difference between two half-cell potentials can be measured.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-044","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":44,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.05 mol L$^{-1}$ solution of an electrolyte has a conductivity of 0.60 S m$^{-1}$ at 298 K. What is its molar conductivity in SI units?","image":"","options":["$12 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","$1.2 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","$1200 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","$120 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$ = 0.60 S m$^{-1}$ and c = 0.05 mol L$^{-1}$.\nConvert concentration: c = 0.05 × 1000 = 50 mol m$^{-3}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa}{c}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{0.60}{50}$ = 0.012 S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Lambda_m$ = $120 \\times 10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$, which equals 120 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-045","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":45,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the cell reaction $2\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\text{(aq)} + 2\\text{I}^-\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + \\text{I}_2\\text{(s)}$, $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.236 V at 298 K. What is the equilibrium constant at 298 K for the same reaction written as $\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\text{(aq)} + \\text{I}^-\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{I}_2\\text{(s)}$? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F}$ = 0.059 V.)","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{8}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{2}$","$2.0 \\times 10^{4}$","$1.0 \\times 10^{4}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ is intensive, so it stays 0.236 V for the halved equation. Now n = 1.\nFormula: $\\log K = \\frac{nE^\\circ}{0.059}$.\nSubstituting: $\\log K = \\frac{1 \\times 0.236}{0.059}$ = 4.0, so $K = 1.0 \\times 10^4$.\nCheck: for the original equation, n = 2 gives $\\log K$ = 8. The new K is the square root of $10^8$, as expected when coefficients are halved.\nUsing n = 2 here gives the tempting wrong value $10^8$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-046","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":46,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the electrode potential of a copper electrode dipped in 0.01 M $CuSO_4$ solution at 298 K. Given: $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu) = 0.34$ V and $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.","image":"","options":["0.281 V","0.399 V","0.222 V","0.311 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ = 0.34$ V, $[Cu^{2+}] = 0.01$ M, n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log\\frac{1}{[Cu^{2+}]}$\nSubstituting: $E = 0.34 - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log 100 = 0.34 - 0.059$\nSo E = 0.281 V. The value is lower than $E^\\circ$ because the ion concentration is below 1 M.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-047","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":47,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Given $E^\\circ$ (V): $Zn^{2+}/Zn$ = −0.76, $Fe^{2+}/Fe$ = −0.44, $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ = +0.34.\nStatement I: A 1 M copper sulphate solution can be stored safely in a zinc vessel.\nStatement II: An iron nail placed in copper sulphate solution gets coated with copper, because $E^\\circ(Fe^{2+}/Fe)$ is lower than $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. For $Zn + Cu^{2+} \\rightarrow Zn^{2+} + Cu$, $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.34 − (−0.76) = 1.10 V > 0. Zinc would dissolve and copper would deposit, so the pot gets corroded.\nStatement II is correct. Iron has the lower reduction potential, so Fe reduces $Cu^{2+}$ with $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.78 V.\nA metal with a lower $E^\\circ$ displaces a metal with a higher $E^\\circ$ from its salt solution.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-048","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":48,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (species) with List-II (description based on the electrochemical series in aqueous solution):\nList-I\n(A) $F_2$\n(B) Li\n(C) $F^-$\n(D) $Li^+$\nList-II\n(I) Weakest oxidising agent\n(II) Strongest reducing agent\n(III) Weakest reducing agent\n(IV) Strongest oxidising agent","image":"","options":["A–IV, B–II, C–III, D–I","A–IV, B–II, C–I, D–III","A–II, B–IV, C–I, D–III","A–III, B–I, C–IV, D–II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"$F_2/F^-$ has the highest $E^\\circ$ (2.87 V). So $F_2$ is the strongest oxidising agent (A–IV) and $F^-$ is the weakest reducing agent (C–III).\n$Li^+/Li$ has the lowest $E^\\circ$ (−3.05 V). So Li is the strongest reducing agent (B–II) and $Li^+$ is the weakest oxidising agent (D–I).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-049","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":49,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"An aqueous solution of KCl is diluted step by step at constant temperature. Which option correctly describes how its conductivity ($\\kappa$) and molar conductivity ($\\Lambda_m$) change?","image":"","options":["Both $\\kappa$ and $\\Lambda_m$ increase","$\\kappa$ decreases while $\\Lambda_m$ increases","$\\kappa$ increases while $\\Lambda_m$ decreases","Both $\\kappa$ and $\\Lambda_m$ decrease"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On dilution, the number of ions per unit volume falls, so $\\kappa$ decreases. This holds for both strong and weak electrolytes.\nBy contrast, $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa V$, where V is the volume containing 1 mol of electrolyte. V rises on dilution.\nThe increase in V more than compensates for the fall in $\\kappa$, so $\\Lambda_m$ increases.\nOption (1) is the tempting wrong choice. It wrongly assumes that $\\kappa$ also rises on dilution.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-050","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":50,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about a Daniell cell, Zn|Zn$^{2+}$(1 M)||Cu$^{2+}$(1 M)|Cu, connected to a variable external source that opposes the cell potential.\n(a) In galvanic mode, the copper electrode is positive and reduction occurs on it.\n(b) When $E_{ext}$ is slightly less than 1.1 V, the reaction Zn + Cu$^{2+}$ → Zn$^{2+}$ + Cu still proceeds.\n(c) When $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V, electrons flow from Cu to Zn through the external circuit.\n(d) When $E_{ext}$ = 1.1 V, [Zn$^{2+}$] keeps increasing slowly.\n(e) In galvanic mode, conventional current flows from Zn to Cu in the external circuit.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(a) Correct: Cu is the cathode, where Cu$^{2+}$ is reduced, and it is the positive electrode.\n(b) Correct: the forward reaction continues until the opposing voltage reaches 1.1 V.\n(c) Correct: the larger external voltage drives electrons from Cu to Zn.\n(d) Incorrect: at 1.1 V the reaction stops altogether, so concentrations do not change.\n(e) Incorrect: in galvanic mode electrons flow Zn → Cu, so current flows Cu → Zn.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-051","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":51,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A column of an electrolyte solution is 2.0 cm long and has a uniform cross-sectional area of 0.50 cm$^2$. Its resistance is found to be 50 $\\Omega$. What is the conductivity of the solution?","image":"","options":["0.08 S m$^{-1}$","8.0 S m$^{-1}$","0.8 S m$^{-1}$","12.5 S m$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: l = 2.0 cm, A = 0.50 cm$^2$, R = 50 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $\\rho = \\frac{RA}{l}$ and $\\kappa = \\frac{1}{\\rho}$.\nSubstituting: $\\rho = \\frac{50 \\times 0.50}{2.0}$ = 12.5 $\\Omega$ cm.\nThen $\\kappa = \\frac{1}{12.5}$ = 0.08 S cm$^{-1}$.\nConverting units: 0.08 × 100 = 8.0 S m$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-052","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":52,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In a Daniell cell, $Zn(s) + Cu^{2+}(aq) \\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(aq) + Cu(s)$, which of the following changes will increase the cell potential at 298 K?","image":"","options":["Increasing $[Zn^{2+}]$ while keeping $[Cu^{2+}]$ constant","Diluting the $CuSO_4$ solution in the cathode compartment","Increasing $[Cu^{2+}]$ while keeping $[Zn^{2+}]$ constant","Using a thicker zinc rod as the anode"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Nernst equation for the cell: $E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]}$.\nIncreasing $[Cu^{2+}]$ lowers Q, so the subtracted term becomes smaller and $E_{cell}$ increases.\nIncreasing $[Zn^{2+}]$ or diluting $Cu^{2+}$ raises Q, so $E_{cell}$ falls.\nZinc is a solid with unit concentration, so the size of the rod has no effect.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-053","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":53,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider a rusting 'cell' on a steel surface at 298 K in which the reaction $2Fe(s) + O_2(g) + 4H^+(aq) \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+}(aq) + 2H_2O(l)$ occurs with $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.67$ V. The water film has pH = 5, $[Fe^{2+}] = 10^{-3}$ M and $p_{O_2} = 1$ bar. Taking $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V, the cell potential is closest to:","image":"","options":["1.46 V","1.88 V","1.26 V","1.38 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $n = 4$, $[H^+] = 10^{-5}$ M, $[Fe^{2+}] = 10^{-3}$ M, $p_{O_2} = 1$ bar.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log\\frac{[Fe^{2+}]^2}{p_{O_2}[H^+]^4}$.\nSubstituting: $Q = \\frac{10^{-6}}{10^{-20}} = 10^{14}$, so $\\log Q = 14$.\n$E = 1.67 - \\frac{0.059}{4} \\times 14 = 1.67 - 0.2065$.\nSo $E \\approx 1.46$ V. Using $n = 2$ instead of 4 would wrongly give 1.26 V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-054","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":54,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Copper metal dissolves in nitric acid even though it does not dissolve in hydrochloric acid.\nReason (R): In nitric acid, copper is oxidised by the hydrogen ions, which are present at a higher concentration than in HCl.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ = +0.34 V, so $H^+$ (0.00 V) cannot oxidise Cu, and Cu does not dissolve in HCl.\nCopper does dissolve in $HNO_3$, however.\nR is false. In nitric acid, Cu is oxidised by the nitrate ion ($E^\\circ$ = 0.97 V for $NO_3^-/NO$), not by $H^+$.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-055","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":55,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A conductivity cell is filled with 0.010 mol $L^{-1}$ KCl solution, whose conductivity at 298 K is 0.141 S $m^{-1}$. The measured resistance is 500 $\\Omega$. What is the cell constant $G^*$?","image":"","options":["0.705 $cm^{-1}$","7.05 $cm^{-1}$","0.0705 $cm^{-1}$","0.282 $cm^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$ = 0.141 S $m^{-1}$ and R = 500 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: $G^* = \\frac{l}{A} = R\\kappa$.\nSubstituting: $G^*$ = 500 × 0.141 = 70.5 $m^{-1}$.\nConvert units: 1 $m^{-1}$ = 0.01 $cm^{-1}$, so 70.5 $m^{-1}$ = 0.705 $cm^{-1}$.\nSo $G^*$ = 0.705 $cm^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-056","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":56,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): In the electrolysis of aqueous NaCl with inert electrodes, chlorine gas is liberated at the anode.\nReason (R): The standard electrode potential for the $O_2/H_2O$ couple (1.23 V) is lower than that for the $Cl_2/Cl^-$ couple (1.36 V).","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: $Cl_2$ is indeed formed at the anode.\nR is true: the values are 1.23 V and 1.36 V.\nHowever, R would predict that water is oxidised in preference to $Cl^-$, because the lower $E^\\circ$ is preferred at the anode. That predicts $O_2$, not $Cl_2$.\n$Cl_2$ actually forms because of the overpotential of oxygen. So R does not explain A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-057","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":57,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The conductivity of a 0.010 mol $L^{-1}$ acetic acid solution at 298 K is $1.65 \\times 10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$. Given: $\\lambda^\\circ(H^+) = 349.6$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(CH_3COO^-) = 40.9$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$. Calculate the dissociation constant of acetic acid.","image":"","options":["$1.86 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol $L^{-1}$","$1.79 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol $L^{-1}$","$1.86 \\times 10^{-4}$ mol $L^{-1}$","$4.23 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol $L^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: c = 0.010 mol $L^{-1}$ and $\\kappa = 1.65 \\times 10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$.\nFormulae: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{c}$, $\\alpha = \\frac{\\Lambda_m}{\\Lambda^\\circ_m}$, $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda_m = 16.5$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$. $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = 349.6 + 40.9 = 390.5$, so $\\alpha = 0.0423$.\n$K_a = \\frac{0.010 \\times (0.0423)^2}{0.9577}$.\nSo $K_a$ = $1.86 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol $L^{-1}$. Option (b) wrongly drops the $(1-\\alpha)$ term.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-058","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":58,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about rusting of iron is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The anodic and cathodic spots can be different regions on the surface of the same iron object.","Electrons released at the anodic spot travel through the water film to the cathodic spot, where they reduce oxygen.","Oxidation of $Fe^{2+}$ to hydrated ferric oxide by atmospheric oxygen produces more $H^+$ ions.","Dissolution of acidic oxides from the atmosphere in the water film can supply $H^+$ for the cathodic reaction."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: one spot of the object behaves as the anode and another spot as the cathode.\n(2) Incorrect: electrons move through the metal itself to the cathodic spot. The water film conducts ions, not electrons.\n(3) Correct: $2Fe^{2+} + 2H_2O + \\frac{1}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3 + 4H^+$, so $H^+$ is regenerated and rusting continues.\n(4) Correct: $CO_2$ and other acidic oxides furnish $H^+$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-059","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":59,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"While measuring the resistance of an electrolytic solution on a Wheatstone bridge, an alternating current (AC) source is used instead of a direct current (DC) source. What is the main reason?","image":"","options":["AC gives a larger current, so the bridge is more sensitive","Prolonged DC causes electrode reactions that change the composition of the solution","Electrolytic solutions cannot conduct direct current at all","AC keeps the temperature of the solution constant"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"NCERT names two problems in measuring the resistance of an ionic solution.\nFirst, passing DC causes electrochemical reactions at the electrodes, so the composition of the solution changes. This is removed by using an AC source.\nSecond, a solution cannot be connected like a wire. This is solved with a conductivity cell.\nOption (3) is wrong because ionic solutions do conduct DC; that conduction is exactly what causes electrolysis.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-060","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":60,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.01 mol L$^{-1}$ solution of acetic acid has a conductivity of 0.016 S m$^{-1}$ at 298 K. Its $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is 390.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$. Find the dissociation constant of acetic acid.","image":"","options":["$1.68 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.75 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.75 \\times 10^{-3}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$4.10 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$ = 0.016 S m$^{-1}$ = $1.6 \\times 10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$ and c = 0.01 mol L$^{-1}$.\nMolar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{c} = \\frac{1.6\\times10^{-4}\\times1000}{0.01}$ = 16 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nDegree of dissociation: $\\alpha = \\frac{\\Lambda_m}{\\Lambda^\\circ_m} = \\frac{16}{390.5}$ = 0.0410.\nDissociation constant: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} = \\frac{0.01(0.0410)^2}{0.959}$ = $1.75 \\times 10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$.\nOption (1) is wrong because it ignores the $(1-\\alpha)$ term.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-061","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":61,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the electrolytic refining of copper is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["The impure copper is made the anode.","Pure copper is deposited on the cathode.","An aqueous copper sulphate solution serves as the electrolyte.","$Cu^{2+}$ ions are reduced at the anode while copper atoms are oxidised at the cathode."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the impure copper anode dissolves as current passes.\n(2) Correct: $Cu^{2+} + 2e^- \\rightarrow Cu$ deposits pure copper at the cathode.\n(3) Correct: the electrolyte is $CuSO_4$ solution.\n(4) Incorrect: the processes are reversed. Copper is oxidised ($Cu \\rightarrow Cu^{2+} + 2e^-$) at the anode, and $Cu^{2+}$ is reduced at the cathode.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-062","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":62,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the galvanic cell represented as Cu(s)|Cu$^{2+}$(aq)||Ag$^{+}$(aq)|Ag(s), which of the following statements is correct?","image":"","options":["Electrons flow through the external wire from the silver electrode to the copper electrode","The silver electrode is the cathode and carries a positive potential","Copper ions are reduced at the left-hand electrode","Conventional current flows in the external circuit from the copper electrode to the silver electrode"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"By convention the anode is written on the left and the cathode on the right, so Cu is the anode and Ag is the cathode.\n(1) Wrong: Cu is oxidised and releases electrons, so electrons flow from Cu to Ag.\n(2) Correct: Ag$^{+}$ is reduced at the Ag electrode. That makes it the cathode, which is positive with respect to the solution.\n(3) Wrong: Cu metal is oxidised at the left electrode. It is not Cu$^{2+}$ being reduced there.\n(4) Wrong: current flows opposite to electron flow, so it goes from Ag to Cu externally.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-063","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":63,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How much charge is needed to reduce 0.2 mol of dichromate ions to $Cr^{3+}$ in acidic solution according to the half reaction $Cr_2O_7^{2-} + 14H^+ + 6e^- \\rightarrow 2Cr^{3+} + 7H_2O$? (Take F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["115800 C","19300 C","57900 C","231600 C"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n($Cr_2O_7^{2-}$) = 0.2 mol. Each ion takes 6 electrons, because each Cr changes from +6 to +3.\nFormula: Q = (moles of ion) × (electrons per ion) × F\nSubstituting: Q = 0.2 × 6 × 96500\nSo Q = 115800 C. Counting only 3 electrons, one Cr's change, gives 57900 C, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-064","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":64,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The potential of a mercury cell (about 1.35 V) stays nearly constant throughout its life. The main reason is that:","image":"","options":["The zinc–mercury amalgam anode never gets consumed","The overall cell reaction involves no ion in solution whose concentration changes during use","The KOH electrolyte is continuously regenerated by the cathode reaction","Mercury(II) oxide acts only as a catalyst and is not consumed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The overall reaction is Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) → ZnO(s) + Hg(l). It involves only solids and a liquid.\nNo dissolved ion's concentration changes during the cell's life, so the Nernst term does not change and the potential stays constant.\nOption (1) is wrong because Zn is oxidised and consumed.\nOption (4) is wrong because HgO is the oxidising agent that gets reduced, not a catalyst.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-065","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":65,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Calculate the emf at 298 K of the cell $Mg(s)|Mg^{2+}(0.01\\,M)||Ag^+(0.001\\,M)|Ag(s)$. Given: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 3.17$ V and $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.","image":"","options":["3.052 V","3.288 V","2.934 V","3.141 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: the reaction is $Mg + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Mg^{2+} + 2Ag$, with n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Mg^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$\nSubstituting: $Q = \\frac{0.01}{(10^{-3})^2} = 10^4$, so $E = 3.17 - 0.0295 \\times 4 = 3.17 - 0.118$\nSo E = 3.052 V. Forgetting the square on $[Ag^+]$ gives 3.141 V; using n = 1 gives 2.934 V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-066","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":66,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (quantity) with List-II (SI unit).\nList-I:\n(A) Resistivity\n(B) Conductivity\n(C) Molar conductivity\n(D) Cell constant\nList-II:\n(I) S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$\n(II) m$^{-1}$\n(III) $\\Omega$ m\n(IV) S m$^{-1}$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Resistivity: $\\rho = RA/l$ gives $\\Omega$ m, so A-III.\n(B) Conductivity: $\\kappa = 1/\\rho$ gives S m$^{-1}$, so B-IV.\n(C) Molar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa/c$ = (S m$^{-1}$)/(mol m$^{-3}$) = S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$, so C-I.\n(D) Cell constant: $l/A$ gives m$^{-1}$, so D-II.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-067","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":67,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Rust that finally appears on an iron object exposed to moist air is chemically:","image":"","options":["Anhydrous ferrous oxide, $FeO$","Hydrated ferric oxide, $Fe_2O_3 \\cdot xH_2O$","Ferrous hydroxide, $Fe(OH)_2$","Iron(II) carbonate, $FeCO_3$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The $Fe^{2+}$ ions formed at the anode are further oxidised by atmospheric oxygen to the +3 state.\nThey separate out as hydrated ferric oxide, $Fe_2O_3 \\cdot xH_2O$, which is rust.\n$FeO$ and $Fe(OH)_2$ contain iron in the +2 state, so they are not the final product.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-068","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":68,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The cell $Ni(s)|Ni^{2+}(0.1\\,M)||Ag^+(x\\,M)|Ag(s)$ shows an emf of 0.9615 V at 298 K. Given $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.05$ V and $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V, find the value of x.","image":"","options":["0.01 M","$1 \\times 10^{-4}$ M","0.056 M","$1 \\times 10^{-3}$ M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: n = 2 and $Q = \\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$.\nFormula: $0.9615 = 1.05 - 0.0295\\log Q$, so $\\log Q = \\frac{0.0885}{0.0295} = 3$ and $Q = 10^3$.\nSubstituting: $\\frac{0.1}{x^2} = 10^3$, giving $x^2 = 10^{-4}$.\nSo x = 0.01 M. Ignoring the square gives $10^{-4}$ M; using n = 1 gives 0.056 M.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-069","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":69,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When aqueous NaCl is electrolysed, hydrogen gas and not sodium metal is obtained at the cathode.\nReason (R): The reduction H$^+$(aq) + e$^-$ → $\\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$(g) has a higher standard electrode potential (0.00 V) than Na$^+$(aq) + e$^-$ → Na(s) (−2.71 V).","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: in aqueous NaCl, the products at the cathode are H$_2$ and OH$^-$, from H$_2$O + e$^-$ → $\\frac{1}{2}$H$_2$ + OH$^-$.\nR is true: at the cathode, the reduction with the higher E° is preferred. 0.00 V is higher than −2.71 V, so H$^+$ (from water) is reduced instead of Na$^+$.\nR therefore directly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-070","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":70,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A 0.1 mol $L^{-1}$ aqueous KCl solution is diluted step by step with water at constant temperature. Which of the following correctly describes how its conductivity ($\\kappa$) and molar conductivity ($\\Lambda_m$) change?","image":"","options":["Both $\\kappa$ and $\\Lambda_m$ decrease","$\\kappa$ decreases but $\\Lambda_m$ increases","$\\kappa$ increases but $\\Lambda_m$ decreases","Both $\\kappa$ and $\\Lambda_m$ increase"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"On dilution, the number of current-carrying ions per unit volume falls, so $\\kappa$ decreases. This is true for both strong and weak electrolytes.\nAt the same time, $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa V$, where V is the volume containing 1 mol of electrolyte. V increases on dilution.\nThe rise in V more than compensates for the fall in $\\kappa$, so $\\Lambda_m$ increases.\nOption (a) is tempting, but it wrongly assumes that $\\Lambda_m$ follows $\\kappa$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-071","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":71,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Electronic conductance of a metal decreases with increase in temperature.\n(ii) Conductivity of an electrolytic solution increases with increase in temperature.\n(iii) The composition of a metallic conductor changes as current passes through it.\n(iv) Conductivity decreases on dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes.\n(v) Conductivity of an ionic solution is measured with a DC source on a Wheatstone bridge, because DC does not alter the solution.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: electronic conductance falls as temperature rises.\n(ii) Correct: ionic conductance rises with temperature.\n(iii) Incorrect: electrons enter at one end and leave at the other, so the metal's composition is unchanged.\n(iv) Correct: on dilution there are fewer ions per unit volume, so conductivity falls.\n(v) Incorrect: DC changes the composition of the solution, so AC (550–5000 Hz) is used.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-072","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":72,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The conductivity of a 0.010 mol L$^{-1}$ acetic acid solution at 298 K is $1.56 \\times 10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$. Given $\\lambda^0(H^+) = 349.6$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^0(CH_3COO^-) = 40.9$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$, what is the degree of dissociation of acetic acid at this concentration?","image":"","options":["0.040","0.045","0.078","0.0040"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa = 1.56\\times10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$, c = 0.010 mol L$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{c}$ and $\\alpha = \\frac{\\Lambda_m}{\\Lambda_m^0}$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{1.56\\times10^{-4}\\times1000}{0.010} = 15.6$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nBy Kohlrausch's law, $\\Lambda_m^0 = 349.6 + 40.9 = 390.5$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nSo $\\alpha = \\frac{15.6}{390.5} \\approx 0.040$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-073","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":73,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Based on the standard electrode potentials in the electrochemical series at 298 K, which species is the most powerful reducing agent in aqueous solution?","image":"","options":["$K(s)$","$Li(s)$","$F^-(aq)$","$Na(s)$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The most negative $E^\\circ$ means the reduced form is most easily oxidised, so it is the strongest reducing agent.\n$E^\\circ(Li^+/Li)$ = −3.05 V is the lowest in the table, so Li is the strongest reductant in water.\nK (−2.93 V) is tempting but its potential is less negative than that of Li.\n$F^-$ is the weakest reducing agent, because $F_2$ has the highest $E^\\circ$ (2.87 V).\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-074","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":74,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Find the emf at 298 K of the cell\n$Ni(s)|Ni^{2+}(0.1\\ M)||Ag^+(0.01\\ M)|Ag(s)$\nGiven: $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.05$ V, $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V, $\\log 1000 = 3$.","image":"","options":["0.99 V","0.96 V","1.14 V","1.05 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the cell reaction is $Ni + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Ni^{2+} + 2Ag$, so n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$.\nSubstituting: $Q = \\frac{0.1}{(0.01)^2} = 1000$, so $E = 1.05 - 0.0295\\times 3 = 1.05 - 0.0885$.\nSo E ≈ 0.96 V.\nUsing n = 1 with $[Ag^+]$ not squared gives 0.99 V, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-075","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":75,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The mercury cell gives a nearly constant potential of about 1.35 V throughout its life. The reason is that:","image":"","options":["the overall cell reaction involves no ion in solution whose concentration changes during use","zinc amalgam does not get oxidised during discharge","the KOH electrolyte is replenished continuously from outside","HgO is the strongest oxidising agent known"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The overall reaction is $Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) \\rightarrow ZnO(s) + Hg(l)$.\nIt involves only solids and a liquid, with no dissolved ion whose concentration changes. By the Nernst equation, the cell potential therefore stays constant.\nZinc is in fact oxidised; it is the reducing agent.\nNothing is fed into the cell from outside; that is a feature of fuel cells.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-076","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":76,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The limiting molar conductivity of acetic acid cannot be obtained by extrapolating the plot of $\\Lambda_m$ versus $c^{1/2}$ to zero concentration.\nReason (R): Even at infinite dilution, a weak electrolyte like acetic acid remains only partially dissociated.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. For a weak electrolyte, $\\Lambda_m$ rises steeply at low concentration, so the curve cannot be extrapolated reliably. $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is obtained using Kohlrausch's law instead.\nR is false. As $c \\to 0$, the weak electrolyte dissociates completely ($\\alpha = 1$). The real difficulty is that conductivity is then too low to measure accurately.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-077","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":77,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A current of 2.0 A is passed through aqueous CuSO$_4$ for 965 s. What mass of copper is deposited at the cathode? (Cu = 63.5 g mol$^{-1}$, F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1.27 g","0.3175 g","0.635 g","0.0635 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: I = 2.0 A, t = 965 s, M = 63.5 g mol$^{-1}$. The reaction Cu$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ → Cu needs n = 2.\nFormula: Q = It, and m = $\\frac{MQ}{nF}$\nSubstituting: Q = 2.0 × 965 = 1930 C, which is 0.02 F. This gives 0.01 mol Cu.\nSo m = 0.01 × 63.5 = 0.635 g\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-078","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":78,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, under standard conditions, the cell $X(s)|X^{2+}(aq)||Cu^{2+}(aq)|Cu(s)$ has emf 0.78 V. The cell $X(s)|X^{2+}(aq)||Y^+(aq)|Y(s)$ has emf 1.24 V. Given $E^\\circ_{Cu^{2+}/Cu} = 0.34$ V, what is $E^\\circ_{Y^+/Y}$?","image":"","options":["0.80 V","1.68 V","0.12 V","−0.80 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: in both cells X is on the left, so X is the anode. $E^\\circ_{Cu^{2+}/Cu}$ = 0.34 V.\nFormula: $E_{cell} = E_{right} - E_{left}$.\nFirst cell: 0.78 = 0.34 − $E_X$, so $E^\\circ_{X^{2+}/X}$ = −0.44 V.\nSecond cell: 1.24 = $E_Y$ − (−0.44), so $E_Y$ = 1.24 − 0.44.\nSo $E^\\circ_{Y^+/Y}$ = 0.80 V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-079","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":79,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the Daniell cell at 298 K, $Zn(s)|Zn^{2+}(aq)||Cu^{2+}(aq)|Cu(s)$, which one of the following changes will increase the cell potential $E_{cell}$?","image":"","options":["Increasing $[Cu^{2+}]$ while keeping $[Zn^{2+}]$ constant","Increasing $[Zn^{2+}]$ while keeping $[Cu^{2+}]$ constant","Diluting the $CuSO_4$ solution while keeping $[Zn^{2+}]$ constant","Increasing both $[Zn^{2+}]$ and $[Cu^{2+}]$ by the same factor"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The Nernst equation for this cell is $E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]}$.\nRaising $[Cu^{2+}]$ makes Q smaller, so less is subtracted and $E_{cell}$ rises.\nRaising $[Zn^{2+}]$ or diluting $CuSO_4$ makes Q larger, so $E_{cell}$ falls.\nChanging both concentrations by the same factor leaves the ratio, and hence $E_{cell}$, unchanged.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-080","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":80,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the Leclanché (dry) cell, the cathode reaction is $MnO_2 + NH_4^+ + e^- \\rightarrow MnO(OH) + NH_3$. What change in the oxidation state of manganese occurs in this reaction?","image":"","options":["+4 to +2","+4 to +3","+3 to +2","+7 to +4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"In $MnO_2$, Mn has an oxidation state of +4 (2 O atoms at −2 each).\nIn $MnO(OH)$, Mn has an oxidation state of +3 (O at −2 and OH at −1).\nOnly one electron is gained per Mn, so Mn is reduced from +4 to +3, as NCERT states.\nOption +4 to +2 would need 2 electrons and would give $Mn^{2+}$, which is not formed here.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-081","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":81,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When the same quantity of electricity is passed through aqueous $AgNO_3$ and aqueous $CuSO_4$ connected in series, the masses of Ag and Cu deposited are in the ratio 108 : 31.75 (Ag = 108, Cu = 63.5).\nStatement II: According to Faraday's second law, the mass of a substance liberated is proportional to its atomic mass, irrespective of the number of electrons needed to reduce its cation.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. Masses are in the ratio of equivalent weights: Ag = 108/1 = 108 and Cu = 63.5/2 = 31.75.\nStatement II is incorrect. Faraday's second law uses chemical equivalent weight, which is atomic mass divided by the number of electrons required to reduce the cation. It does not use atomic mass alone.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-082","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":82,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The cell $Mg(s)|Mg^{2+}(0.1\\ M)||Ag^+(x\\ M)|Ag(s)$ has $E_{cell} = 3.0225$ V at 298 K. Given $E^\\circ_{cell} = 3.17$ V and $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V, what is the concentration x of $Ag^+$?","image":"","options":["$1.0 \\times 10^{-2}$ M","$1.0 \\times 10^{-3}$ M","$1.0 \\times 10^{-6}$ M","$1.78 \\times 10^{-2}$ M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: the cell reaction is $Mg + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Mg^{2+} + 2Ag$, so n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Mg^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$.\nSubstituting: $3.17 - 3.0225 = 0.1475 = 0.0295\\log Q$, so $\\log Q = 5$ and $Q = 10^5$.\nThen $\\frac{0.1}{x^2} = 10^5$, giving $x^2 = 10^{-6}$.\nSo x = $1.0\\times10^{-3}$ M.\nForgetting to square $[Ag^+]$ gives $10^{-6}$ M, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-083","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":83,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A Daniell cell is connected to an external source of 1.5 V that opposes the cell potential. A steady current of 0.50 A flows for 1930 s. What mass of zinc is deposited on the zinc electrode? (Atomic mass of Zn = 65.4 g mol$^{-1}$, F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["0.327 g","0.654 g","0.318 g","0.164 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E_{ext}$ = 1.5 V > 1.1 V, so the cell acts as an electrolytic cell. Zn$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ → Zn occurs at the zinc electrode. I = 0.50 A, t = 1930 s.\nFormula: Q = It, and moles of Zn = Q/(2F).\nSubstituting: Q = 0.50 × 1930 = 965 C, which is 0.01 F. Moles of Zn = 0.01/2 = 0.005 mol.\nMass = 0.005 × 65.4 = 0.327 g.\nOption (2) forgets the 2 electrons per Zn. Option (3) is the mass of Cu dissolved.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-084","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":84,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When an external opposing potential greater than 1.1 V is applied to a Daniell cell, zinc gets deposited on the zinc electrode.\nStatement II: Under this condition, the zinc electrode continues to act as the anode of the cell.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. When $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V the reaction runs in reverse: Zn$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ → Zn at the zinc electrode, and copper dissolves.\nStatement II is incorrect. Reduction now happens at the zinc electrode, so it is the cathode. Oxidation of Cu makes the copper electrode the anode.\nThe device now works as an electrolytic cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-085","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":85,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The limiting molar conductivity of acetic acid cannot be found by extrapolating its $\\Lambda_m$ versus $c^{1/2}$ curve to zero concentration.\nReason (R): For a weak electrolyte, $\\Lambda_m$ rises very steeply at low concentrations because its degree of dissociation increases on dilution.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. For acetic acid, the curve rises sharply near zero concentration, so it cannot be extrapolated reliably. Its $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is therefore obtained from Kohlrausch law.\nR is true. On dilution, the degree of dissociation increases, so the number of ions per mole rises and $\\Lambda_m$ climbs steeply.\nThis steep, non-linear rise is exactly why extrapolation fails, so R explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-086","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":86,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The Leclanché cell is a primary cell.\n(ii) In the dry cell, the zinc container acts as the cathode.\n(iii) Ammonia formed at the cathode of the dry cell combines with Zn$^{2+}$ to form $[Zn(NH_3)_4]^{2+}$.\n(iv) The nickel–cadmium cell has a longer life than the lead storage cell but is more expensive to manufacture.\n(v) The H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell converts fuel energy into electricity with an efficiency of only about 40%.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the dry cell cannot be reused once dead.\n(ii) Incorrect: the zinc container is the anode; the graphite rod is the cathode.\n(iii) Correct: NH$_3$ complexes Zn$^{2+}$ to form $[Zn(NH_3)_4]^{2+}$.\n(iv) Correct, as stated in NCERT.\n(v) Incorrect: fuel cells are about 70% efficient; about 40% is the efficiency of thermal plants.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-087","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":87,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) The conductivity of an electrolytic solution increases with rise in temperature.\n(ii) Electronic (metallic) conductance increases with rise in temperature.\n(iii) Conductivity decreases on dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes.\n(iv) Molar conductivity of a strong electrolyte decreases on dilution.\n(v) Passing direct current through an ionic solution for a long time can change its composition.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: ionic conductance increases with temperature.\n(ii) Incorrect: metallic conductance decreases as temperature rises.\n(iii) Correct: dilution reduces the number of ions per unit volume.\n(iv) Incorrect: $\\Lambda_m$ increases on dilution, slowly for strong electrolytes.\n(v) Correct: this is why AC is used when measuring conductivity.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-088","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":88,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: If a solution of a strong electrolyte in a given conductivity cell is replaced by a solution of the same electrolyte at double the concentration (same temperature), the measured resistance becomes exactly half.\nStatement II: The molar conductivity of a strong electrolyte decreases as its concentration increases.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"In a given cell, $R = G^*/\\kappa$ and $\\kappa = \\Lambda_m c$.\nIf c doubles, $\\kappa$ would double only if $\\Lambda_m$ stayed constant.\nStatement II is correct: for strong electrolytes, $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda_m^\\circ - A c^{1/2}$, so $\\Lambda_m$ falls as c rises.\nTherefore $\\kappa$ rises less than twofold, and R falls to slightly more than half, not exactly half. Statement I is incorrect.\nTable 2.3 confirms this: a tenfold rise in KCl concentration (0.01 to 0.1 M) raises $\\kappa$ only from 0.141 to 1.29 S m$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-089","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":89,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): A galvanic cell in which both electrodes dip in the same electrolyte solution does not require a salt bridge.\nReason (R): In a galvanic cell, the salt bridge internally connects the electrolytes of the two separate half-cells.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. The text states that when both electrodes dip in the same electrolyte, no salt bridge is required.\nR is true. The salt bridge's role is to join the two separate electrolytes internally and complete the circuit.\nWhen there is only one common electrolyte, nothing separate needs joining. So R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-090","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":90,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: When a lead storage battery is recharged, the PbSO$_4$ on the electrode that acted as the anode during discharge is converted back into Pb.\nStatement II: During recharging, the battery works as an electrolytic cell, and this electrode undergoes reduction.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"During discharge, the Pb anode is oxidised: $Pb + SO_4^{2-} \\rightarrow PbSO_4 + 2e^-$.\nOn charging, current is passed in the opposite direction, so the reactions are reversed. PbSO$_4$ on the anode becomes Pb, and PbSO$_4$ on the cathode becomes PbO$_2$. Statement I is correct.\nConverting PbSO$_4$ (Pb at +2) to Pb (0) is a reduction, driven by an external source, so the battery acts as an electrolytic cell. Statement II is correct.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-091","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":91,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the cell $Ni(s)|Ni^{2+}(aq)||Ag^+(aq)|Ag(s)$ at 298 K, which expression correctly gives the cell potential?","image":"","options":["$E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - 0.059\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]}$","$E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$","$E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ag^+]^2}{[Ni^{2+}]}$","$E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"The cell reaction is $Ni + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Ni^{2+} + 2Ag$, so n = 2. The same n must be used for both electrodes.\nQ = $[Ni^{2+}]/[Ag^+]^2$, because solids have unit concentration.\nOption (4) omits the square on $[Ag^+]$.\nOption (3) inverts Q.\nOption (1) uses n = 1.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-092","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":92,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (system electrolysed using inert Pt electrodes) with List-II (product described):\nList-I\n(A) Molten NaCl\n(B) Aqueous NaCl\n(C) Dilute $H_2SO_4$ (anode product)\n(D) Concentrated $H_2SO_4$ (anode product)\nList-II\n(P) $S_2O_8^{2-}$ ions\n(Q) Sodium metal at cathode\n(R) $O_2$ gas\n(S) $H_2$ at cathode, with $OH^-$ left in solution","image":"","options":["A-Q, B-S, C-R, D-P","A-S, B-Q, C-R, D-P","A-Q, B-S, C-P, D-R","A-Q, B-R, C-S, D-P"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) Molten NaCl has only $Na^+$ as cation, so Na is formed at the cathode (Q).\n(B) In aqueous NaCl, water is reduced in preference to $Na^+$ ($E^0$ 0.00 V vs –2.71 V). This gives $H_2$ and leaves $OH^-$ (S).\n(C) In dilute $H_2SO_4$, oxidation of water to $O_2$ is preferred (R).\n(D) At high acid concentration, $SO_4^{2-}$ is oxidised to $S_2O_8^{2-}$ (P).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-093","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":93,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"An H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell operates with a steady current of 9.65 A for 1 hour. What mass of water is produced in the cell?\n(Cathode: $O_2 + 2H_2O + 4e^- \\rightarrow 4OH^-$; Anode: $2H_2 + 4OH^- \\rightarrow 4H_2O + 4e^-$; F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$; molar mass of H$_2$O = 18 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["1.62 g","3.24 g","6.48 g","0.81 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: I = 9.65 A, t = 3600 s.\nCharge: Q = 9.65 × 3600 = 34740 C, so moles of e$^-$ = 34740/96500 = 0.36 mol.\nThe overall reaction $2H_2 + O_2 \\rightarrow 2H_2O$ transfers 4 mol e$^-$ for every 2 mol of water. That is 0.5 mol H$_2$O per mol e$^-$.\nWater formed = 0.18 mol, so mass = 0.18 × 18 = 3.24 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-094","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":94,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: During electrolysis of sulphuric acid with platinum electrodes, O$_2$ is liberated at the anode from dilute acid, whereas S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$ is formed at the anode from concentrated acid.\nStatement II: At the anode, the oxidation with the lower standard electrode potential is always the one that occurs, irrespective of the concentrations of the species or any overpotential.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct: NCERT states that water oxidation (E° = 1.23 V) is preferred in dilute H$_2$SO$_4$, but SO$_4^{2-}$ oxidation to S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$ (E° = 1.96 V) is preferred at higher concentrations.\nStatement II is incorrect: standard potentials must be replaced by Nernst electrode potentials, which depend on concentration, and overpotential can also change the outcome.\nAn example is aqueous NaCl, where Cl$_2$ forms instead of O$_2$ because of the overpotential of oxygen.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-095","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":95,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The potential of a mercury cell remains nearly constant (about 1.35 V) throughout its life.\nStatement II: In the cathode reaction of a Leclanché (dry) cell, manganese is reduced from the +4 to the +2 oxidation state.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. The overall reaction $Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) \\rightarrow ZnO(s) + Hg(l)$ involves no ion in solution whose concentration changes, so the voltage stays constant.\nStatement II is incorrect. The cathode reaction is $MnO_2 + NH_4^+ + e^- \\rightarrow MnO(OH) + NH_3$. Mn changes from +4 to +3, not +2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-096","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":96,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A conductivity cell filled with 0.1 mol $L^{-1}$ KCl ($\\kappa$ = 1.29 S $m^{-1}$) has a resistance of 200 $\\Omega$. The same cell is then filled with a 0.05 mol $L^{-1}$ solution of an electrolyte X and placed in a Wheatstone bridge. The bridge balances when $R_1$ = 400 $\\Omega$ (variable resistance), $R_3$ = 100 $\\Omega$ and $R_4$ = 300 $\\Omega$, where $R_2$ is the cell. What is the molar conductivity of X?","image":"","options":["43.0 S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$","387 S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$","4.30 S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$","430 S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Step 1, cell constant: $G^* = R\\kappa$ = 200 × 1.29 = 258 $m^{-1}$.\nStep 2, resistance of the cell with X: $R_2 = \\frac{R_1R_4}{R_3} = \\frac{400 \\times 300}{100}$ = 1200 $\\Omega$.\nStep 3, conductivity of X: $\\kappa = \\frac{G^*}{R_2} = \\frac{258}{1200}$ = 0.215 S $m^{-1}$.\nStep 4, concentration: c = 0.05 × 1000 = 50 mol $m^{-3}$.\nStep 5, molar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa}{c} = \\frac{0.215}{50}$ = $4.3 \\times 10^{-3}$ S $m^2$ $mol^{-1}$. Multiplying by $10^4$ gives 43.0 S $cm^2$ $mol^{-1}$.\nCommon error: using $R_2 = \\frac{R_1R_3}{R_4}$ gives 387.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-097","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":97,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A hydrogen electrode, with $H_2$ gas at 1 bar, is placed in a solution of pH 3 at 298 K. What is its electrode potential? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.)","image":"","options":["$-0.177$ V","$+0.177$ V","$-0.059$ V","$-0.531$ V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $H^+ + e^- \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}H_2$, $E^\\circ = 0$, n = 1, $[H^+] = 10^{-3}$ M.\nFormula: $E = 0 - 0.059\\log\\frac{1}{[H^+]} = -0.059\\,pH$\nSubstituting: $E = -0.059 \\times 3$\nSo E = $-0.177$ V. The potential is negative because the solution is less acidic than standard (1 M $H^+$).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-098","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":98,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"An external voltage opposing the Daniell cell is raised slowly until it equals exactly 1.1 V. Which statement describes the cell at this point?","image":"","options":["Zinc dissolves and copper deposits at the maximum rate","Copper dissolves and zinc deposits","No current flows and no chemical reaction takes place","Current flows but no chemical change occurs"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"When $E_{ext}$ = 1.1 V, the opposing voltage exactly balances the cell emf.\nThere is no net driving force, so electrons do not flow and the redox reaction stops altogether.\nOption (1) describes the cell when $E_{ext}$ < 1.1 V. Option (2) describes it when $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V.\nOption (4) is impossible here: in this cell, current through the circuit always accompanies electrode reactions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-099","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":99,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (condition on the cell) with List-II (inference about the cell reaction).\nList-I:\n(A) $E^\\circ_{cell} > 0$\n(B) $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0$\n(C) $E^\\circ_{cell} < 0$\n(D) $E_{cell} = 0$\nList-II:\n(I) Q = K, the system is at equilibrium\n(II) K = 1, $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = 0$\n(III) K > 1, $\\Delta_rG^\\circ < 0$\n(IV) K < 1, $\\Delta_rG^\\circ > 0$","image":"","options":["A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Use $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell} = -RT\\ln K$.\n(A) A positive $E^\\circ$ gives a negative $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ and K > 1, so A matches III.\n(B) $E^\\circ$ = 0 gives $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ = 0 and $\\ln K$ = 0, i.e., K = 1, so B matches II.\n(C) A negative $E^\\circ$ gives a positive $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ and K < 1, so C matches IV.\n(D) $E_{cell}$ = 0 (not $E^\\circ$) means Q = K, i.e., equilibrium, so D matches I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-100","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":100,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A lead storage battery supplies a steady current of 2.0 A for 9650 s. What mass of H$_2$SO$_4$ is consumed during this discharge?\n(Overall reaction: $Pb + PbO_2 + 2H_2SO_4 \\rightarrow 2PbSO_4 + 2H_2O$; F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$; molar mass of H$_2$SO$_4$ = 98 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["9.8 g","19.6 g","39.2 g","4.9 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: I = 2.0 A, t = 9650 s.\nCharge: Q = It = 19300 C, so moles of electrons = 19300/96500 = 0.2 mol.\nStoichiometry: each electrode half-reaction involves 2e$^-$, so the overall reaction transfers 2 mol e$^-$ and consumes 2 mol H$_2$SO$_4$. Hence 1 mol H$_2$SO$_4$ is consumed per mol e$^-$.\nMoles of H$_2$SO$_4$ = 0.2 mol, so mass = 0.2 × 98 = 19.6 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-101","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":101,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the Hydrogen Economy are correct?\n(i) Combustion of hydrogen produces only water.\n(ii) Hydrogen for this purpose should be produced by splitting water using solar energy.\n(iii) $CO_2$ from burning fossil fuels causes the greenhouse effect, which motivates the shift to hydrogen.\n(iv) In the hydrogen economy, hydrogen is burnt in thermal power plants to raise steam, which is more efficient than fuel cells.\n(v) Both electrolysis of water and use of hydrogen in fuel cells are based on electrochemical principles.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: burning hydrogen gives only water, so no carbonaceous pollution is produced.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT says hydrogen must come from splitting water using solar energy.\n(iii) Correct: $CO_2$ from fossil fuels is causing the greenhouse effect.\n(iv) Incorrect: fuel cells (about 70% efficient) are better than thermal plants (about 40%), and the vision relies on fuel cells.\n(v) Correct: both technologies are electrochemical.\nSo 4 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-102","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":102,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"One litre of aqueous NaCl solution (initially neutral) is electrolysed with inert electrodes using a current of 0.965 A for 1000 s. Assume the volume stays 1 L, the $Cl_2$ formed escapes completely, and the current efficiency is 100%. What is the pH of the solution at the end? (F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$, $K_w = 10^{-14}$, log 2 = 0.30)","image":"","options":["2","11.7","12","13"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: I = 0.965 A, t = 1000 s, so Q = It = 965 C.\nMoles of electrons = 965/96500 = 0.01 mol.\nThe cathode reaction $H_2O + e^- \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}H_2 + OH^-$ forms one $OH^-$ per electron, giving 0.01 mol $OH^-$ in 1 L.\n$[OH^-] = 10^{-2}$ M, so pOH = 2 and pH = 14 − 2 = 12.\nTaking 0.005 mol $OH^-$ would wrongly give pH 11.7.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-103","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":103,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In the bridge arrangement used to measure the resistance of an electrolyte solution, the bridge balances when the variable resistance $R_1$ = 200 $\\Omega$, $R_3$ = 100 $\\Omega$ and $R_4$ = 150 $\\Omega$. What is the unknown resistance $R_2$ of the conductivity cell?","image":"","options":["300 $\\Omega$","75 $\\Omega$","133.3 $\\Omega$","750 $\\Omega$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $R_1$ = 200 $\\Omega$, $R_3$ = 100 $\\Omega$, $R_4$ = 150 $\\Omega$.\nFormula: at balance (no current through the detector), $R_2 = \\frac{R_1 R_4}{R_3}$.\nSubstituting: $R_2$ = (200 × 150)/100.\nSo $R_2$ = 300 $\\Omega$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-104","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":104,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The standard electrode potentials of $Zn^{2+}/Zn$ and $Ag^+/Ag$ are $-0.76$ V and $+0.80$ V respectively. What is the standard emf of the cell Zn(s)|$Zn^{2+}$(1 M)||$Ag^+$(1 M)|Ag(s)?","image":"","options":["0.04 V","1.56 V","$-1.56$ V","0.84 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{right}$ (Ag) = +0.80 V, $E^\\circ_{left}$ (Zn) = $-0.76$ V.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{right} - E^\\circ_{left}$.\nSubstituting: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.80 − (−0.76) V.\nSo $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 1.56 V.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-105","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":105,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A Daniell cell ($E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 1.1 V, both ion concentrations 1 M) is connected to an external source that applies an opposing voltage of 1.5 V. Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["Electrons now flow in the external circuit from the copper rod to the zinc rod","Zinc is deposited on the zinc electrode","Copper dissolves from the copper electrode","The zinc electrode continues to act as the anode, because zinc is the more reactive metal"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"When $E_{ext}$ exceeds 1.1 V, the reaction reverses and the device works as an electrolytic cell.\n(1) Correct: electrons flow from Cu to Zn.\n(2) Correct: $Zn^{2+}$ is reduced, so zinc is deposited.\n(3) Correct: Cu is oxidised to $Cu^{2+}$ and dissolves.\n(4) Incorrect: reduction now occurs at zinc, so zinc is the cathode. The anode is defined by where oxidation occurs, not by the metal's reactivity.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-106","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":106,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: Copper metal does not liberate hydrogen gas from dilute hydrochloric acid.\nStatement II: The standard electrode potential of $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ is positive (0.34 V), so $H^+$ ions cannot oxidise copper under standard conditions.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct, and Statement II is the correct explanation of Statement I","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct, but Statement II is not the correct explanation of Statement I","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is true: Cu does not dissolve in HCl.\nStatement II is true: $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu)$ = +0.34 V, so $Cu^{2+}$ is reduced more easily than $H^+$.\nThe reverse change, $H^+$ oxidising Cu, therefore cannot occur. This is exactly why no $H_2$ is evolved, so II explains I.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-107","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":107,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A Daniell cell is made of a zinc rod in ZnSO$_4$ solution and a copper rod in CuSO$_4$ solution, with the two solutions joined by a salt bridge. Both Zn$^{2+}$ and Cu$^{2+}$ are at 1 mol dm$^{-3}$ and the temperature is 298 K. What is the electrical potential of the cell?","image":"","options":["0.34 V","0.76 V","1.10 V","0.42 V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Under standard conditions, $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$.\nThe cathode is Cu$^{2+}$/Cu with $E^\\circ = +0.34$ V. The anode is Zn$^{2+}$/Zn with $E^\\circ = -0.76$ V.\nSo $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0.34 - (-0.76) = 1.10$ V.\nThe value 0.42 V comes from wrongly adding $-0.76$ to 0.34 instead of subtracting it. The values 0.34 V and 0.76 V are only single electrode potentials.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-108","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":108,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following correctly describes the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) as used to measure standard electrode potentials?","image":"","options":["A platinum electrode coated with platinum black dips in a solution with 1 M $H^+$, and pure $H_2$ at 1 bar is bubbled through it. It is assigned zero potential at all temperatures.","A bright platinum electrode dips in a solution with 1 M $H^+$, and $H_2$ at 1 atm is bubbled through it. It is assigned zero potential only at 298 K.","A platinum electrode coated with platinum black dips in 1 M NaOH, and $H_2$ at 1 bar is bubbled through it. It is assigned zero potential at all temperatures.","A graphite electrode dips in 0.1 M HCl, and $H_2$ at 1 bar is bubbled through it. It is assigned a potential of 0.059 V at 298 K."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"NCERT construction: platinum coated with platinum black is dipped in an acidic solution with $[H^+]$ = 1 M, and pure $H_2$ at 1 bar is bubbled through it.\nBy convention its potential is taken as zero at all temperatures.\nOption 2 fails on two counts: zero is not restricted to 298 K, and the electrode is platinised, not bright.\nOption 3 uses a basic solution, not an acidic one.\nOption 4 uses the wrong electrode, the wrong concentration and the wrong potential.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-109","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":109,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following factors affect the conductivity of an electrolytic (ionic) solution, as described in NCERT?\n(i) Nature of the electrolyte\n(ii) Size of the ions and their solvation\n(iii) Nature of the solvent and its viscosity\n(iv) Concentration of the electrolyte\n(v) Temperature\n(vi) Number of valence electrons per atom of the electrode metal","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i)–(v) Correct: NCERT lists all five as factors for ionic conductance. Conductivity increases with temperature.\n(vi) Incorrect: the number of valence electrons per atom affects electronic (metallic) conductance, not ionic conductance.\nCount = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-110","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":110,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (electrolysis setup) with List-II (observation).\nList-I:\n(A) Molten NaCl, inert electrodes\n(B) Aqueous NaCl, inert electrodes\n(C) Concentrated H$_2$SO$_4$, platinum anode\n(D) Aqueous CuSO$_4$, copper electrodes\nList-II:\n(i) Peroxodisulphate ion (S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$) formed at anode\n(ii) Hydrogen gas evolved at cathode\n(iii) Anode itself dissolves as Cu$^{2+}$\n(iv) Sodium metal formed at cathode","image":"","options":["A-ii, B-iv, C-i, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-iii, D-i","A-iv, B-i, C-ii, D-iii","A-iv, B-ii, C-i, D-iii"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) Molten NaCl contains only Na$^+$ as the cation, so Na$^+$ is reduced to Na metal → (iv).\n(B) In aqueous NaCl, water is reduced in preference to Na$^+$, so H$_2$ is evolved → (ii).\n(C) In concentrated H$_2$SO$_4$, the anode reaction 2SO$_4^{2-}$ → S$_2$O$_8^{2-}$ + 2e$^-$ is preferred → (i).\n(D) A copper anode is a reactive electrode and is oxidised to Cu$^{2+}$ → (iii).\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-111","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":111,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, the limiting molar conductivities of HCl, HCOONa and NaCl are 425.9, 104.7 and 126.4 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ respectively. Calculate $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ for methanoic acid (HCOOH).","image":"","options":["447.6 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","657.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","404.2 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","194.8 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCl) = 425.9, $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCOONa) = 104.7 and $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(NaCl) = 126.4 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCOOH) = $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCl) + $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCOONa) $-$ $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(NaCl). The $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ contributions cancel.\nSubstituting: 425.9 + 104.7 $-$ 126.4.\nSo $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(HCOOH) = 404.2 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nCheck: $\\lambda^\\circ(H^+) + \\lambda^\\circ(HCOO^-)$ = 349.6 + 54.6 = 404.2.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-112","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":112,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, the limiting molar conductivities of $CaCl_2$, $K_2SO_4$ and KCl are 271.6, 307.0 and 149.8 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ respectively. Using only these data, find the limiting molar conductivity of $CaSO_4$.","image":"","options":["428.8 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","578.6 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","35.4 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","279.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m(CaCl_2) = \\lambda^\\circ_{Ca^{2+}} + 2\\lambda^\\circ_{Cl^-}$, $\\Lambda^\\circ_m(K_2SO_4) = 2\\lambda^\\circ_{K^+} + \\lambda^\\circ_{SO_4^{2-}}$ and $\\Lambda^\\circ_m(KCl) = \\lambda^\\circ_{K^+} + \\lambda^\\circ_{Cl^-}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m(CaSO_4) = \\Lambda^\\circ_m(CaCl_2) + \\Lambda^\\circ_m(K_2SO_4) - 2\\Lambda^\\circ_m(KCl)$. This cancels 2 $K^+$ and 2 $Cl^-$.\nSubstituting: 271.6 + 307.0 - 2(149.8).\nSo $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ = 279.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$, which agrees with 119.0 + 160.0 from Table 2.4.\nOption (a) subtracts KCl only once.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-113","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":113,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about measuring the conductivity of ionic solutions are correct?\n(i) The electrodes of a conductivity cell are platinum coated with platinum black.\n(ii) The cell constant has the dimension of length$^{-1}$.\n(iii) The cell constant of a given cell changes when the electrolyte filled in it is changed.\n(iv) The oscillator feeding the bridge supplies AC in the audio frequency range of about 550 to 5000 cycles per second.\n(v) A headphone can act as the detector, and the bridge is balanced when no current passes through it.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: finely divided Pt is deposited electrochemically on the electrodes.\n(ii) Correct: $G^* = l/A$, which has units of m$^{-1}$ or cm$^{-1}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: $G^*$ depends only on the electrode separation and area. The electrolyte only changes R and $\\kappa$.\n(iv) Correct, as stated in NCERT.\n(v) Correct: the detector P may be a headphone, and balance means zero current through it.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-114","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":114,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: The equilibrium constant $K_c$ for the Daniell cell reaction $\\text{Zn(s)} + \\text{Cu}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} \\rightleftharpoons \\text{Zn}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + \\text{Cu(s)}$ at 298 K is about $2 \\times 10^{37}$.\nStatement II: At equilibrium in this system, the concentration of Cu$^{2+}$ ions is negligibly small compared with that of Zn$^{2+}$ ions.\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Statement I is correct. From $E^\\circ_{cell} = \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log K_c$ = 1.1 V, we get $\\log K_c$ ≈ 37.29, so $K_c \\approx 2 \\times 10^{37}$.\nStatement II is correct. $K_c = \\frac{[\\text{Zn}^{2+}]}{[\\text{Cu}^{2+}]}$ is enormous, so at equilibrium [Cu$^{2+}$] is tiny compared with [Zn$^{2+}$].\nThis means the reaction goes practically to completion.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-115","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":115,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for the representation of a galvanic cell.\nList-I:\n(A) Single vertical line $|$\n(B) Double vertical line $||$\n(C) Half-cell written on the left\n(D) Conventional current in the external circuit\nList-II:\n(I) Salt bridge joining the two electrolytes\n(II) Boundary between electrode and its electrolyte\n(III) Flows from cathode to anode\n(IV) Anode, where oxidation occurs","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-IV, D-III","A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) A single line separates the metal from its electrolyte, so A matches II.\n(B) A double line represents the salt bridge, so B matches I.\n(C) The anode (oxidation) is always written on the left, so C matches IV.\n(D) Electrons move from anode to cathode, so conventional current flows from cathode to anode. D matches III.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-116","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":116,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Given $E^\\circ$ (V): $Zn^{2+}/Zn$ = −0.76, $Fe^{2+}/Fe$ = −0.44, $Ni^{2+}/Ni$ = −0.25, $Sn^{2+}/Sn$ = −0.14, $Al^{3+}/Al$ = −1.66, $Mg^{2+}/Mg$ = −2.36.\nWhich metal can displace nickel from aqueous $NiSO_4$ but CANNOT displace zinc from aqueous $ZnSO_4$?","image":"","options":["Fe","Sn","Al","Mg"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"To displace $Ni^{2+}$, a metal needs $E^\\circ$ lower than −0.25 V. To fail with $Zn^{2+}$, its $E^\\circ$ must be higher than −0.76 V.\nFe (−0.44 V) lies between these limits, so it fits.\nSn (−0.14 V) cannot reduce $Ni^{2+}$.\nAl and Mg have $E^\\circ$ below −0.76 V, so they displace zinc as well.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-117","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":117,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the circuit of a Daniell cell is kept closed for a long time, the voltmeter reading eventually drops to zero.\nReason (R): At equilibrium the reaction quotient equals $K_c$, so the Nernst equation gives $E_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cell} - \\frac{2.303RT}{nF}\\log K_c = 0$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. As the cell runs, [Zn$^{2+}$] rises and [Cu$^{2+}$] falls, and the cell voltage keeps decreasing until it reaches zero.\nR is true. At equilibrium Q = $K_c$, and $E^\\circ_{cell} = \\frac{2.303RT}{nF}\\log K_c$, so the two terms cancel and $E_{cell}$ = 0.\nR explains A: zero voltage is the electrochemical signature of equilibrium.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-118","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":118,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A Daniell cell (Zn | $ZnSO_4$ || $CuSO_4$ | Cu, both solutions 1 M) is connected to an external source. The source's voltage opposes the cell and is slowly raised until it is greater than 1.1 V. Which observation is then expected?","image":"","options":["Electrons flow from Zn rod to Cu rod, and zinc dissolves","No current flows and no chemical reaction occurs","Zinc is deposited on the zinc electrode and copper dissolves from the copper electrode","Copper is deposited on the copper electrode and the zinc electrode is unaffected"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Below 1.1 V, the cell still works as a galvanic cell: Zn dissolves and Cu deposits.\nAt exactly 1.1 V, no current flows and no reaction occurs.\nAbove 1.1 V, the reaction is driven in reverse. The cell now acts as an electrolytic cell: electrons flow from Cu to Zn, Zn is deposited and Cu dissolves.\nOption (1) describes the case $E_{ext} < 1.1$ V, not the case asked.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-119","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":119,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following non-metals is classified among electronic conductors in NCERT?","image":"","options":["Glass","Teflon","Graphite","Copper(II) oxide"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"NCERT notes that certain non-metals are electronically conducting, such as carbon-black, graphite and some organic polymers.\nIn Table 2.2, graphite is placed among the conductors.\nGlass and Teflon are insulators, with very low conductivity.\nCuO is listed as a semiconductor, so it is not a conductor.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-120","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":120,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the concentration cell at 298 K:\n$Zn(s)|Zn^{2+}(0.001\\ M)||Zn^{2+}(0.1\\ M)|Zn(s)$\nWhat is its emf? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.)","image":"","options":["0.0295 V","0.118 V","0.059 V","$-0.059$ V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: both electrodes are Zn, so $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0$ and n = 2.\nFormula: the net change is $Zn^{2+}(0.1\\ M) \\rightarrow Zn^{2+}(0.001\\ M)$, so $E = 0 - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Zn^{2+}]_{anode}}{[Zn^{2+}]_{cathode}}$.\nSubstituting: $E = -0.0295\\log\\frac{0.001}{0.1} = -0.0295\\times(-2)$.\nSo E = 0.059 V. The emf is positive because the dilute side acts as anode.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-121","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":121,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II ($R$ = resistance of the solution in the cell, $\\kappa$ = conductivity, $c$ = concentration, $G^*$ = cell constant):\nList-I\n(A) Cell constant\n(B) Conductivity\n(C) Molar conductivity\n(D) $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ of $CaCl_2$\nList-II\n(I) $\\frac{\\kappa}{c}$\n(II) $R\\kappa$\n(III) $\\lambda^\\circ_{Ca^{2+}} + 2\\lambda^\\circ_{Cl^-}$\n(IV) $\\frac{G^*}{R}$","image":"","options":["A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I","A-I, B-IV, C-II, D-III","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(A) The cell constant is found with a solution of known conductivity: $G^* = l/A = R\\kappa$. This is II.\n(B) Once $G^*$ is known, $\\kappa = G^*/R$. This is IV.\n(C) By definition, $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa/c$. This is I.\n(D) Kohlrausch's law gives $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = \\nu_+\\lambda^\\circ_+ + \\nu_-\\lambda^\\circ_-$. For $CaCl_2$, $\\nu_- = 2$. This is III.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-122","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":122,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["It uses a platinum electrode coated with platinum black.","Pure hydrogen gas at 1 bar is bubbled through a solution in which $[H^+]$ = 1 M.","Its potential is taken as zero only at 298 K and has a non-zero value at other temperatures.","It is represented as Pt(s)|$H_2$(g)|$H^+$(aq)."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: the Pt electrode is coated with platinum black.\n(2) Correct: unit concentration of both forms means $H_2$ at 1 bar and $H^+$ at 1 M.\n(3) Incorrect: by convention the SHE is assigned zero potential at all temperatures, not only at 298 K.\n(4) Correct: this is the standard notation for the SHE.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-123","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":123,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A mercury cell used in hearing aids and watches gives an almost constant potential of about 1.35 V throughout its life. Which of the following best explains this?","image":"","options":["The KOH electrolyte is continuously regenerated by the cathode reaction, so its concentration rises steadily","The overall cell reaction, Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) $\\rightarrow$ ZnO(s) + Hg(l), involves no ion in solution whose concentration changes during use","The zinc–mercury amalgam anode is not oxidised during discharge, so the anode potential never changes","It is a secondary cell, and it recharges itself while the device is switched off"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Key fact: the overall reaction of the mercury cell is Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) $\\rightarrow$ ZnO(s) + Hg(l). It contains only solids and a liquid.\nOH$^-$ is used up at the anode and released in equal amount at the cathode, so its net concentration stays the same.\nThe Nernst equation therefore has no concentration term that can change, and E stays at about 1.35 V.\n(1) fails because KOH is not consumed overall and its concentration does not rise. (3) fails because zinc is the species that gets oxidised. (4) fails because the mercury cell is a primary cell.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-124","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":124,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"During electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride solution using inert electrodes, which product is formed at the cathode?","image":"","options":["Sodium metal","Hydrogen gas","Oxygen gas","Chlorine gas"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Two reductions compete at the cathode: $Na^+ + e^- \\rightarrow Na$ with $E^\\circ = -2.71$ V, and $H^+ + e^- \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}H_2$ with $E^\\circ = 0.00$ V.\nThe reaction with the higher $E^\\circ$ is preferred, so $H_2$ is liberated. The net cathode reaction is $H_2O + e^- \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2}H_2 + OH^-$.\nSodium metal is obtained only from molten NaCl, where $Na^+$ is the only cation present.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-125","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":125,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using $E^\\circ(\\text{Fe}^{3+}/\\text{Fe}^{2+})$ = 0.77 V and $E^\\circ(\\text{I}_2/\\text{I}^-)$ = 0.54 V, calculate $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ at 298 K for the reaction written as $\\text{Fe}^{3+}\\text{(aq)} + \\text{I}^-\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{I}_2\\text{(s)}$. (F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["$+22.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-44.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-126.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-22.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: Fe$^{3+}$ is reduced, so it is the cathode. I$^-$ is oxidised, so it is the anode.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell} = E^\\circ_{cathode} - E^\\circ_{anode}$ = 0.77 − 0.54 = 0.23 V.\nAs written, one electron is transferred, so n = 1. Then $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ$.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -1 \\times 96500 \\times 0.23 = -22195$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nAdding the two potentials would wrongly give −126.4 kJ, and using n = 2 would give −44.4 kJ.\nSo $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ ≈ $-22.2$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-126","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":126,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For a strong electrolyte, $\\Lambda_m$ is plotted on the y-axis against $c^{1/2}$ on the x-axis, giving a straight line. What do the intercept and the slope of this line represent?","image":"","options":["Intercept = $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$; slope = $-A$","Intercept = $-A$; slope = $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$","Intercept = $\\kappa$; slope = $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$","Intercept = $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$; slope = $+A$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For strong electrolytes, $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - A c^{1/2}$.\nComparing this with $y = mx + c$, the intercept at $c^{1/2} = 0$ is $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$.\nThe slope is $-A$, because $\\Lambda_m$ falls as concentration rises.\nOption (4) is wrong because it reverses the sign of the slope.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-127","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":127,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A concentration cell is set up as $Ag(s)|Ag^+(0.001\\,M)||Ag^+(0.1\\,M)|Ag(s)$. What is its emf at 298 K? (Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V.)","image":"","options":["0.059 V","0.118 V","0.236 V","$-0.118$ V"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: both electrodes are identical, so $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0$ and n = 1.\nThe net change is $Ag^+(0.1\\,M) \\rightarrow Ag^+(0.001\\,M)$.\nFormula: $E = 0 - 0.059\\log\\frac{[Ag^+]_{anode}}{[Ag^+]_{cathode}}$\nSubstituting: $E = -0.059\\log\\frac{0.001}{0.1} = -0.059 \\times (-2)$\nSo E = 0.118 V. The value is positive because the more concentrated side acts as the cathode.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-128","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":128,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (cell) with List-II (electrolyte used).\nList-I:\n(A) Lead storage battery\n(B) Mercury cell\n(C) Leclanché cell\n(D) $H_2$–$O_2$ fuel cell (Apollo)\nList-II:\n(I) Moist paste of $NH_4Cl$ and $ZnCl_2$\n(II) 38% solution of $H_2SO_4$\n(III) Paste of KOH and ZnO\n(IV) Concentrated aqueous NaOH","image":"","options":["A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II","A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(A) Lead storage battery uses 38% $H_2SO_4$, so A-II.\n(B) Mercury cell uses a paste of KOH and ZnO, so B-III.\n(C) Dry cell uses a moist $NH_4Cl$ + $ZnCl_2$ paste, so C-I.\n(D) In the fuel cell, $H_2$ and $O_2$ are bubbled through porous carbon electrodes into concentrated aqueous NaOH, so D-IV.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-129","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":129,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Statement I: In a galvanic cell, the cathode acquires a negative potential with respect to the solution.\nStatement II: In the external circuit of a galvanic cell, electrons flow from the anode to the cathode, and current flows in the opposite direction.","image":"","options":["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Statement I is incorrect. Reduction occurs at the cathode, and it has a positive potential with respect to the solution. The anode, where oxidation occurs, is the negative electrode.\nStatement II is correct. Electrons move from the negative electrode (anode) to the positive electrode (cathode). Conventional current flows opposite to electron flow.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-130","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":130,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about electronically conducting polymers is correct?","image":"","options":["Polyacetylene acquires metallic lustre and conductivity when exposed to iodine vapours.","They are composed mainly of metal atoms, so they are heavier than ordinary metals.","They conduct electricity by the movement of ions, like electrolytic solutions.","Their discovery earned MacDiarmid, Heeger and Shirakawa the Nobel Prize in Physics in 1977."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: polyacetylene made from acetylene becomes lustrous and conducting when exposed to iodine vapour.\n(2) Incorrect: these polymers are made of C and H, with occasional N, O or S, so they are lighter than metals.\n(3) Incorrect: they are electronically conducting, not ionically conducting.\n(4) Incorrect: the discovery was made in 1977, but the Nobel Prize was in Chemistry, in 2000.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-131","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":131,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about the lead storage battery and the H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell:\n(i) During discharge of the lead storage battery, PbSO$_4$ is formed at both the anode and the cathode.\n(ii) The electrolyte in the lead storage battery is about 38% sulphuric acid solution.\n(iii) The concentration of H$_2$SO$_4$ in the lead storage battery increases as the battery discharges.\n(iv) On recharging, PbSO$_4$ on the electrode that acted as cathode during discharge is converted back into PbO$_2$.\n(v) The H$_2$–O$_2$ fuel cell used in the Apollo space programme had dilute sulphuric acid as its electrolyte.\nHow many of the above statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: at the anode Pb $\\rightarrow$ PbSO$_4$, and at the cathode PbO$_2$ $\\rightarrow$ PbSO$_4$.\n(ii) Correct: NCERT states that a 38% H$_2$SO$_4$ solution is used.\n(iii) Incorrect: the overall reaction Pb + PbO$_2$ + 2H$_2$SO$_4$ $\\rightarrow$ 2PbSO$_4$ + 2H$_2$O uses up acid and forms water, so the acid concentration falls.\n(iv) Correct: charging reverses the reaction, converting PbSO$_4$ into Pb at one electrode and PbO$_2$ at the other.\n(v) Incorrect: H$_2$ and O$_2$ were bubbled through porous carbon electrodes into concentrated aqueous NaOH.\nSo three statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-132","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":132,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the cell reaction $\\text{Cu(s)} + 2\\text{Ag}^+\\text{(aq)} \\rightarrow \\text{Cu}^{2+}\\text{(aq)} + 2\\text{Ag(s)}$, the standard cell potential at 298 K is 0.46 V. What is the standard Gibbs energy change for the reaction as written? (Take F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$.)","image":"","options":["$-88.78$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-44.39$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-177.56$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$+88.78$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.46 V. Two electrons are transferred, since Cu goes to Cu$^{2+}$, so n = 2.\nFormula: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_rG^\\circ = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 0.46 = -88780$ J mol$^{-1}$.\nThe value is negative because a positive $E^\\circ$ means the reaction is spontaneous. Using n = 1 would give the tempting $-44.39$ kJ.\nSo $\\Delta_rG^\\circ$ = $-88.78$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-133","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":133,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Copper metal does not dissolve in dilute hydrochloric acid to liberate hydrogen.\nReason (R): The standard electrode potential of the $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ couple is positive, so $H^+$ ions cannot oxidise copper under standard conditions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true. Copper does not displace hydrogen from HCl.\nR is true. $E^\\circ(Cu^{2+}/Cu) = +0.34$ V, so $Cu^{2+}$ is reduced more easily than $H^+$. The reverse change, $H^+$ oxidising Cu, is therefore not feasible.\nR directly explains A. (In nitric acid, Cu is oxidised by the nitrate ion, not by $H^+$.)\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-134","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":134,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A charge of 9650 C is passed through molten aluminium oxide dissolved in cryolite. What mass of aluminium is deposited at the cathode? (Al = 27 g mol$^{-1}$, F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["2.7 g","1.8 g","0.9 g","0.3 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: Q = 9650 C. The cathode reaction is $Al^{3+} + 3e^- \\rightarrow Al$.\nMoles of electrons = 9650/96500 = 0.1 mol.\nMoles of Al = 0.1/3 = 0.0333 mol.\nMass = 0.0333 × 27 = 0.9 g. Ignoring the 3 electrons per ion would give 2.7 g, which is wrong.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-135","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":135,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The conductance of a copper wire decreases when its temperature is raised.\nReason (R): Electronic conductance depends on the number of valence electrons per atom of the metal.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A.","A is true but R is false.","A is false but R is true."],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"A is true: electronic (metallic) conductance decreases as temperature increases.\nR is true: the number of valence electrons per atom is one of the factors that affect electronic conductance.\nHowever, R does not explain why conductance falls on heating. It only lists a different factor, which has nothing to do with temperature.\nSo both statements are true, but R is not the explanation of A.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-136","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":136,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): During electrolysis of aqueous sodium chloride with inert electrodes, chlorine gas rather than oxygen is liberated at the anode.\nReason (R): Under standard conditions, oxidation of $Cl^-$ to $Cl_2$ is thermodynamically more favourable than oxidation of water to $O_2$.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A is true. $Cl_2$ is the anode product in the electrolysis of brine.\nR is false. $E^\\circ$ values are 1.36 V for $Cl_2/Cl^-$ and 1.23 V for $O_2/H_2O$. The process with the lower $E^\\circ$ (oxidation of water) is thermodynamically preferred.\n$Cl_2$ forms instead because of the overpotential for oxygen evolution.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-137","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":137,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): For the reaction $Ni(s) + 2Ag^+(aq) \\rightarrow Ni^{2+}(aq) + 2Ag(s)$, writing it as $2Ni(s) + 4Ag^+(aq) \\rightarrow 2Ni^{2+}(aq) + 4Ag(s)$ does not change the $E_{cell}$ calculated from the Nernst equation.\nReason (R): When the equation is doubled, n doubles and Q gets squared, so the term $\\frac{0.059}{n}\\log Q$ remains unchanged.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: $E_{cell}$ is an intensive property and does not depend on how the equation is balanced. (In contrast, $\\Delta_rG$ does change.)\nR is true: doubling gives n' = 2n and Q' = $Q^2$.\nSo $\\frac{0.059}{2n}\\log Q^2 = \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log Q$.\n$E^\\circ$ is also unchanged, so R correctly explains why $E_{cell}$ stays the same.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-138","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":138,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the Nernst equation at 298 K are correct?\n(i) At equilibrium, $E_{cell} = 0$ and $E^\\circ_{cell} = \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log K_c$.\n(ii) A hydrogen electrode (1 bar $H_2$) in a solution of pH 7 has a potential of about $-0.413$ V.\n(iii) In the expression for an $M^{n+}/M$ electrode, the concentration of the solid metal M is taken as unity.\n(iv) In a Daniell cell, diluting the $CuSO_4$ solution tenfold (with $[Zn^{2+}]$ unchanged) increases $E_{cell}$ by 0.0295 V.\n(v) A concentration cell made of two identical electrodes has $E^\\circ_{cell} = 0$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: at equilibrium Q = $K_c$ and E = 0, which gives $E^\\circ = \\frac{0.059}{n}\\log K_c$.\n(ii) Correct: $E = -0.059 \\times 7 = -0.413$ V.\n(iii) Correct: as shown in NCERT, the concentration of a pure solid is taken as 1.\n(iv) Incorrect: lowering $[Cu^{2+}]$ raises Q, so $E_{cell}$ decreases by 0.0295 V.\n(v) Correct: the two standard electrode potentials are equal, so they cancel.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-139","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":139,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (material) with List-II (category based on conductivity):\nList-I\n(a) Teflon\n(b) Silicon\n(c) Graphite\n(d) Certain ceramic mixed oxides near 150 K\nList-II\n(i) Superconductor\n(ii) Electronic conductor\n(iii) Semiconductor\n(iv) Insulator","image":"","options":["(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(i), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iv)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) Teflon has a conductivity of about $10^{-18}$ S m$^{-1}$, so it is an insulator: (iv).\n(b) Silicon has an intermediate conductivity of $1.5 \\times 10^{-2}$ S m$^{-1}$, so it is a semiconductor: (iii).\n(c) Graphite is a non-metal that conducts electronically: (ii).\n(d) Some ceramics and mixed oxides show superconductivity at temperatures as high as 150 K: (i).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-140","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":140,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (feature of galvanic cell representation) with List-II (what it signifies).\nList-I\n(A) Single vertical line ( | )\n(B) Double vertical line ( || )\n(C) Half-cell written on the left\n(D) Potential difference between electrodes when no current is drawn\nList-II\n(i) Salt bridge connecting the two electrolytes\n(ii) Electromotive force (emf) of the cell\n(iii) Boundary between metal electrode and its electrolyte solution\n(iv) Anode, where oxidation occurs\nChoose the correct option.","image":"","options":["A-(iii), B-(i), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(i), B-(iii), C-(iv), D-(ii)","A-(iii), B-(i), C-(ii), D-(iv)","A-(iv), B-(i), C-(iii), D-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A)-(iii): a single vertical line separates the metal from its electrolyte solution, e.g. Zn(s)|Zn$^{2+}$(aq).\n(B)-(i): a double vertical line shows that the two electrolytes are joined by a salt bridge.\n(C)-(iv): by convention the anode (oxidation half-cell) is written on the left and the cathode on the right, so $E_{cell} = E_{right} - E_{left}$ comes out positive.\n(D)-(ii): the cell potential measured when no current is drawn is called the emf of the cell.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-141","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":141,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) In commercial production of metals, a current of 50,000 A corresponds to about 0.518 F of charge per second.\n(ii) Sodium and magnesium are produced industrially by electrolysis of their aqueous chloride solutions.\n(iii) Aluminium is produced by electrolysis of aluminium oxide in the presence of cryolite.\n(iv) By Faraday's second law, the masses of different substances liberated by the same charge are proportional to their chemical equivalent weights.\n(v) Reduction of 1 mol of Mg$^{2+}$ to Mg requires 1 F of charge.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: 50000 C s$^{-1}$ ÷ 96500 C mol$^{-1}$ ≈ 0.518 F per second.\n(ii) Incorrect: Na and Mg are obtained from their fused (molten) chlorides, not aqueous solutions.\n(iii) Correct: Al is extracted from Al$_2$O$_3$ with cryolite.\n(iv) Correct: this is the statement of the second law.\n(v) Incorrect: Mg$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ → Mg needs 2 F per mole.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-142","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":142,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is a secondary (rechargeable) cell?","image":"","options":["Leclanché dry cell","Mercury cell","Nickel–cadmium cell","Hydrogen–oxygen fuel cell"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"A secondary cell can be recharged by passing current through it in the opposite direction.\nThe Ni–Cd cell is a secondary cell, as is the lead storage battery.\nThe dry cell and the mercury cell are primary cells: their reaction occurs only once.\nThe fuel cell is not recharged. It runs only as long as reactants are supplied continuously.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-143","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":143,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II for a Daniell cell connected to an external source that opposes the cell potential.\nList-I:\n(A) $E_{ext}$ < 1.1 V\n(B) $E_{ext}$ = 1.1 V\n(C) $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V\n(D) Zinc electrode when the cell works as a galvanic cell\nList-II:\n(I) No flow of electrons or current\n(II) Negative electrode\n(III) Copper deposits on the copper rod\n(IV) Current flows from Zn to Cu in the external circuit","image":"","options":["A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-I, C-III, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I","A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(A) $E_{ext}$ < 1.1 V: the normal reaction continues, Zn dissolves and Cu deposits, so A-III.\n(B) $E_{ext}$ = 1.1 V: the voltages balance and nothing flows, so B-I.\n(C) $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V: electrons are pushed from Cu to Zn, so current flows from Zn to Cu, giving C-IV.\n(D) In galvanic mode Zn is oxidised and is the negative electrode, so D-II.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-144","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":144,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements about the conductance of electrolytic solutions is correct?","image":"","options":["$\\Lambda_m^\\circ$ of a weak electrolyte such as acetic acid is obtained by extrapolating the $\\Lambda_m$ versus $c^{1/2}$ plot to zero concentration","The constant A in $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda_m^\\circ - A c^{1/2}$ has the same value for NaCl and $MgSO_4$ in water at a given temperature","Molar conductivity of a strong electrolyte decreases slowly on dilution","Conductivity decreases on dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(1) Wrong: for weak electrolytes, $\\Lambda_m$ rises steeply at low concentration, so extrapolation fails. Kohlrausch law is used instead.\n(2) Wrong: A depends on the electrolyte type. NaCl is a 1-1 electrolyte and $MgSO_4$ is 2-2, so their A values differ.\n(3) Wrong: $\\Lambda_m$ of a strong electrolyte increases slowly on dilution.\n(4) Correct: on dilution there are fewer ions per unit volume, so $\\kappa$ falls for all electrolytes.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-145","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":145,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, the measured emf of the cell Pt|$H_2$(1 bar)|$H^+$(1 M)||$Ni^{2+}$(1 M)|Ni is −0.25 V. The emf of the cell Pt|$H_2$(1 bar)|$H^+$(1 M)||$Ag^+$(1 M)|Ag is +0.80 V. Calculate $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ for the reaction Ni(s) + 2$Ag^+$(aq) → $Ni^{2+}$(aq) + 2Ag(s). (F = 96500 C $mol^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["−202.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$","−101.3 kJ $mol^{-1}$","−106.2 kJ $mol^{-1}$","+202.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: SHE is the left electrode with 0 V, so $E^\\circ(Ni^{2+}/Ni)$ = −0.25 V and $E^\\circ(Ag^+/Ag)$ = +0.80 V.\nFormula: $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.80 − (−0.25) = 1.05 V, and $\\Delta_r G^\\circ = -nFE^\\circ_{cell}$ with n = 2.\nSubstituting: $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ = −2 × 96500 × 1.05 = −202650 J $mol^{-1}$.\nSo $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ ≈ −202.7 kJ $mol^{-1}$.\nOption (3) results from wrongly computing 0.80 − 0.25 = 0.55 V.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-146","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":146,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In a diagram of a working Daniell cell, no external opposing voltage is applied. The zinc rod is on the left and the copper rod is on the right, joined by a wire through a voltmeter. In the external wire, which way do electrons and conventional current flow?","image":"","options":["Electrons flow from Cu to Zn; current flows from Zn to Cu","Electrons flow from Zn to Cu; current flows from Cu to Zn","Electrons and current both flow from Zn to Cu","Neither flows until the salt bridge is removed"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Zinc is oxidised at the anode, so electrons are released there and the Zn rod is the negative electrode.\nThese electrons travel through the wire to the Cu rod (cathode), where they reduce Cu$^{2+}$.\nConventional current always flows opposite to electron flow, so it goes from Cu to Zn.\nOption (3) ignores this opposite direction. Removing the salt bridge would break the circuit and stop the flow, not start it.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-147","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":147,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about products of electrolysis are correct?\n(i) Molten NaCl gives sodium at the cathode and chlorine at the anode.\n(ii) Aqueous NaCl with inert electrodes gives $H_2$ at the cathode.\n(iii) Aqueous NaCl with inert electrodes gives $O_2$ at the anode, since water has the lower $E^\\circ$ for oxidation.\n(iv) Dilute $H_2SO_4$ with inert electrodes gives $O_2$ at the anode.\n(v) Concentrated $H_2SO_4$ gives $S_2O_8^{2-}$ at the anode.\n(vi) Aqueous $CuSO_4$ with copper electrodes: copper dissolves at the anode and deposits at the cathode.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: only $Na^+$ and $Cl^-$ are present.\n(ii) Correct: $H_2O + e^- \\to \\frac{1}{2}H_2 + OH^-$ is preferred over $Na^+$ reduction (E° 0.00 V vs –2.71 V).\n(iii) Incorrect: the overpotential of oxygen makes $Cl_2$ form at the anode.\n(iv) Correct and (v) Correct: as stated in NCERT.\n(vi) Correct: this is the principle of copper refining.\nCount = 5.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-148","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":148,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following statements about measuring the conductivity of an electrolytic solution:\n(i) The electrodes of a conductivity cell are made of platinum coated with platinum black.\n(ii) The cell constant has the dimension of length.\n(iii) The cell constant is normally found by directly measuring the distance between the electrodes and their area.\n(iv) When the Wheatstone bridge is balanced, the unknown resistance is $R_2 = \\frac{R_1R_4}{R_3}$.\n(v) The bridge is fed by an oscillator that supplies AC in the audio-frequency range of about 550 to 5000 cycles per second.\nHow many of these statements are correct?","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the electrodes are platinised Pt, with finely divided Pt deposited on them.\n(ii) Incorrect: $G^* = \\frac{l}{A}$, so its dimension is $length^{-1}$.\n(iii) Incorrect: measuring l and A directly is inconvenient and unreliable. $G^*$ is found from the resistance of a KCl solution whose conductivity is known.\n(iv) Correct: at balance, $R_2 = \\frac{R_1R_4}{R_3}$.\n(v) Correct: the oscillator supplies AC at 550–5000 cycles per second.\nSo 3 statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-149","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":149,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Conductivity decreases on dilution for both strong and weak electrolytes.\n(ii) $MgSO_4$ and $ZnSO_4$, being 2-2 electrolytes, have the same value of the constant A in $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - Ac^{1/2}$ in water at a given temperature.\n(iii) For acetic acid, the increase in $\\Lambda_m$ on dilution is mainly due to an increase in its degree of dissociation.\n(iv) $\\Lambda^\\circ_m(NaBr) - \\Lambda^\\circ_m(NaCl)$ is about 23.4 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ at 298 K.\n(v) At infinite dilution, a weak electrolyte dissociates completely, so $\\alpha \\to 1$.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: on dilution, the number of ions per unit volume falls in every case.\n(ii) Correct: all electrolytes of one type have the same A.\n(iii) Correct: for a weak electrolyte, $\\alpha$, and hence the number of ions per mole, rises on dilution.\n(iv) Incorrect: the NaBr - NaCl difference is about 1.8. The value 23.4 is the KX - NaX difference.\n(v) Correct: as c tends to 0, $\\alpha$ tends to 1.\nSo four statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-150","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":150,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In a Daniell cell (Zn | ZnSO$_4$ || CuSO$_4$ | Cu, both solutions 1 M), an external voltage $E_{ext}$ opposing the cell is slowly increased until it becomes greater than 1.1 V. Which of the following describes the cell once $E_{ext} > 1.1$ V?","image":"","options":["Electrons flow from Zn rod to Cu rod; zinc dissolves and copper is deposited","Electrons flow from Cu rod to Zn rod; zinc is deposited and copper dissolves","No current flows and no chemical reaction occurs","Both zinc and copper electrodes dissolve, and hydrogen gas is evolved"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"For $E_{ext} < 1.1$ V the cell works as a galvanic cell: Zn dissolves and Cu is deposited.\nAt $E_{ext} = 1.1$ V no current flows and no reaction occurs.\nFor $E_{ext} > 1.1$ V the reaction is reversed. The device now works as an electrolytic cell.\nElectrons flow from Cu to Zn, so zinc is deposited at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves at the copper electrode.\nOption (1) describes the case $E_{ext} < 1.1$ V, not this one.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-151","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":151,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) For a Daniell cell, $E_{cell}$ increases when $[Zn^{2+}]$ is increased.\n(ii) When a galvanic cell reaches equilibrium, $E_{cell} = 0$ and Q equals $K_c$.\n(iii) Writing the Daniell cell reaction with all coefficients doubled does not change the calculated $E_{cell}$.\n(iv) The potential of a hydrogen electrode with $H_2$ at 1 bar becomes more negative as the pH of the solution increases.\n(v) In the Nernst equation for $M^{n+} + ne^- \\rightarrow M(s)$, the concentration of solid M is taken as zero.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Incorrect: a higher $[Zn^{2+}]$ raises Q and lowers $E_{cell}$.\n(ii) Correct: the voltmeter reads zero and the concentrations stop changing.\n(iii) Correct: n doubles and Q is squared, so $\\frac{0.059}{n}\\log Q$ is unchanged, since $E_{cell}$ is intensive.\n(iv) Correct: $E = -0.059\\,pH$.\n(v) Incorrect: the concentration of a solid is taken as unity, not zero.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-152","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":152,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the following factors:\n(i) nature of the electrolyte added\n(ii) size of the ions produced and their solvation\n(iii) viscosity of the solvent\n(iv) concentration of the electrolyte\n(v) temperature\n(vi) number of valence electrons per atom\n(vii) nature and structure of the metal\nHow many of these factors affect the conductivity of an electrolytic (ionic) solution?","image":"","options":["3","4","5","7"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Factors (i) to (v) affect ionic conductivity: the nature of the electrolyte, the size and solvation of the ions, the nature and viscosity of the solvent, the concentration and the temperature.\nFactors (vi) and (vii) affect electronic (metallic) conductance, not ionic conductance.\nSo the count is 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-153","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":153,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The molar conductivity of a solution is 150 S cm$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$. What is this value in S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$?","image":"","options":["1.5 S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$","0.15 S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$","$1.5 \\times 10^{6}$ S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$","0.015 S m$^{2}$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Given: $\\Lambda_m$ = 150 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nConversion: 1 cm$^2$ = $10^{-4}$ m$^2$, so 1 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ = $10^{-4}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nSubstituting: 150 × $10^{-4}$ = 0.015 S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (3) comes from multiplying by $10^4$ instead of dividing.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-154","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":154,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following is NOT an example of corrosion?","image":"","options":["Tarnishing of silver articles","Development of a green coating on copper and bronze","Rusting of an iron gate","Deposition of copper on the cathode during electrolysis of $CuSO_4$ solution"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["4"],"explanation":"Corrosion is the slow oxidation of a metal surface to its oxides or other salts.\nRusting of iron, tarnishing of silver and the green coating on copper and bronze are all NCERT examples of corrosion.\nDeposition of copper at the cathode is a reduction ($Cu^{2+} + 2e^- \\rightarrow Cu$) driven by an external source. It is not corrosion.\nAnswer: (4)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-155","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":155,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the cell $Ni(s)|Ni^{2+}(0.01\\ M)||Ag^+(x\\ M)|Ag(s)$ at 298 K, $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.05$ V and the measured emf is 0.991 V. Take $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V. What is the concentration x of $Ag^+$ ions?","image":"","options":["0.1 M","0.01 M","0.001 M","0.0316 M"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: cell reaction $Ni + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Ni^{2+} + 2Ag$, so n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Ni^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$.\nSubstituting: $0.991 = 1.05 - 0.0295\\log\\frac{0.01}{x^2}$, so $\\log\\frac{0.01}{x^2} = 2$.\nThen $\\frac{0.01}{x^2} = 100$, giving $x^2 = 10^{-4}$.\nSo x = 0.01 M.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-156","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":156,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"In a galvanic cell, which of the following correctly describes the anode?","image":"","options":["Oxidation occurs at it, and it has a negative potential with respect to the solution","Reduction occurs at it, and it has a positive potential with respect to the solution","Oxidation occurs at it, and it has a positive potential with respect to the solution","Reduction occurs at it, and it has a negative potential with respect to the solution"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"In a galvanic cell, the half-cell where oxidation takes place is called the anode.\nThe metal releases electrons there, so the anode has a negative potential with respect to the solution.\nThe cathode is where reduction occurs, and it has a positive potential.\nOption (3) is tempting because electrolysis conventions are often mixed up. In a galvanic cell, however, the anode is negative.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-157","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":157,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The cell $Pt(s)|H_2(g, 1\\,bar)|HA(0.1\\,M)||H^+(1\\,M)|H_2(g, 1\\,bar)|Pt(s)$ has an emf of 0.177 V at 298 K, where HA is a weak monobasic acid. Taking $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V and assuming $(0.1 - [H^+]) \\approx 0.1$, find the dissociation constant $K_a$ of HA.","image":"","options":["$1 \\times 10^{-5}$","$1 \\times 10^{-6}$","$1 \\times 10^{-4}$","$1 \\times 10^{-3}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"The right electrode is the SHE, with E = 0. The left electrode has $E = -0.059\\,pH$.\n$E_{cell} = E_R - E_L = 0.059\\,pH = 0.177$, so pH = 3 and $[H^+] = 10^{-3}$ M.\nFor HA, $K_a = \\frac{[H^+]^2}{c} = \\frac{(10^{-3})^2}{0.1}$.\nSo $K_a = 1 \\times 10^{-5}$. Taking $K_a = [H^+]$ wrongly gives $10^{-3}$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-158","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":158,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using Kohlrausch's law and the limiting ionic molar conductivities $\\lambda^\\circ$(K$^+$) = 73.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^\\circ$(SO$_4^{2-}$) = 160.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ at 298 K, the limiting molar conductivity $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ of K$_2$SO$_4$ is:","image":"","options":["233.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","307.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","467.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","153.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda^\\circ$(K$^+$) = 73.5 and $\\lambda^\\circ$(SO$_4^{2-}$) = 160.0, both in S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = \\nu_+\\lambda^\\circ_+ + \\nu_-\\lambda^\\circ_-$. K$_2$SO$_4$ gives 2 K$^+$ and 1 SO$_4^{2-}$.\nSubstituting: $2(73.5) + 160.0 = 147.0 + 160.0$.\nSo $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ = 307.0 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-159","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":159,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Consider the cell at 298 K:\nZn(s) | $Zn^{2+}$(0.1 M) || $Ag^+$(0.01 M) | Ag(s)\nGiven $E^0(Zn^{2+}/Zn) = -0.76$ V, $E^0(Ag^+/Ag) = 0.80$ V, $\\frac{2.303RT}{F} = 0.059$ V and F = 96500 C mol$^{-1}$. What is $\\Delta_r G$ for the cell reaction $Zn + 2Ag^+ \\rightarrow Zn^{2+} + 2Ag$ under these conditions?","image":"","options":["$-284.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-301.1$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-295.4$ kJ mol$^{-1}$","$-266.9$ kJ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Given: $E^0_{cell} = 0.80 - (-0.76) = 1.56$ V and n = 2.\nFormula: $E = E^0 - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Ag^+]^2}$, then $\\Delta_r G = -nFE$.\nSubstituting: Q = $\\frac{0.1}{(0.01)^2} = 10^3$, so E = 1.56 – 0.0295 × 3 = 1.4715 V.\n$\\Delta_r G = -2 \\times 96500 \\times 1.4715 \\approx -284.0$ kJ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (2) uses $E^0$ instead of E. Option (3) forgets to square $[Ag^+]$.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-160","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":160,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) Sodium is produced by electrolysis of fused sodium chloride.\n(ii) Magnesium is produced by electrolysis of aqueous magnesium chloride.\n(iii) Aluminium is produced by electrolysis of aluminium oxide in the presence of cryolite.\n(iv) Na, Mg and Al are made electrolytically because no suitable chemical reducing agents are available for their cations.\n(v) A current of 50,000 A corresponds to about 0.518 F of charge per second.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: fused NaCl gives Na and $Cl_2$.\n(ii) Incorrect: Mg is obtained from fused $MgCl_2$. In aqueous solution, water would be reduced to $H_2$ instead.\n(iii) Correct: this is the NCERT statement on aluminium.\n(iv) Correct: no suitable chemical reducing agents exist for these cations.\n(v) Correct: 50000/96500 ≈ 0.518 F per second.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-161","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":161,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"When the resistance of an electrolytic solution is measured in a conductivity cell, an alternating current (AC) source is used instead of a direct current (DC) source. What is the main reason?","image":"","options":["DC flowing for a long time causes electrode reactions that change the composition of the solution","AC raises the temperature of the solution and so increases its conductivity","DC cannot flow through a solution that contains ions","AC makes the cell constant independent of the electrode area"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Key fact: Passing DC through an ionic solution for a long time causes electrochemical reactions at the electrodes. These reactions change the solution's composition, so the measured resistance would drift.\nAC keeps reversing direction, which avoids this problem.\nWhy the tempting options fail: DC can flow through an ionic solution, so option (3) is wrong. Heating the solution is not the purpose of AC, so option (2) is wrong. The cell constant is fixed by the electrode geometry, not by the type of current, so option (4) is wrong.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-162","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":162,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements about the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE) are correct?\n(i) It uses a platinum electrode coated with platinum black.\n(ii) The pressure of hydrogen gas is maintained at 1 bar.\n(iii) The concentration of $H^+$ ions in the solution is 1 M.\n(iv) It is assigned zero potential only at 298 K.\n(v) Platinum takes part in the electrode reaction.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Pt is coated with platinum black.\n(ii) Correct: $H_2$ is maintained at 1 bar.\n(iii) Correct: $[H^+]$ is 1 M.\n(iv) Incorrect: SHE is assigned zero potential at all temperatures.\n(v) Incorrect: Pt is inert. It only provides a surface for the reaction and conducts electrons.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-163","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":163,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): The potential of a mercury cell remains nearly constant (about 1.35 V) throughout its life.\nReason (R): The overall cell reaction, $Zn(Hg) + HgO(s) \\rightarrow ZnO(s) + Hg(l)$, involves no ion in solution whose concentration changes during use.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"A is true: NCERT states that the mercury cell gives about 1.35 V, which stays constant during its life.\nR is true: the overall reaction contains only solids and liquid Hg. There is no dissolved ion in it.\nBy the Nernst equation, the cell potential changes only through concentration terms in Q. With no such terms, E stays constant, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-164","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":164,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): An iron sheet coated with zinc remains protected from rusting even after the zinc layer gets scratched.\nReason (R): Zinc has a more negative standard electrode potential than iron, so zinc is oxidised in preference to iron.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"Checking R: $E^\\circ(Zn^{2+}/Zn) = -0.76$ V and $E^\\circ(Fe^{2+}/Fe) = -0.44$ V. Zinc is the stronger reducing agent, so R is true.\nChecking A: when the coating is scratched, zinc acts as a sacrificial anode. It corrodes itself and saves the iron, so A is true.\nThe protection exists precisely because Zn is oxidised preferentially, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-165","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":165,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): When the external opposing potential applied to a Daniell cell exceeds 1.1 V, the copper electrode becomes the anode.\nReason (R): Under this condition, copper metal is oxidised to Cu$^{2+}$ ions and dissolves from the copper electrode.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"When $E_{ext}$ > 1.1 V, the reverse reaction is forced: Cu + Zn$^{2+}$ → Cu$^{2+}$ + Zn.\nR is true: Cu → Cu$^{2+}$ + 2e$^-$ takes place at the copper electrode, so copper dissolves.\nA is true: the anode is defined as the electrode where oxidation occurs, so copper is now the anode.\nR states exactly why copper becomes the anode, so R correctly explains A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-166","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":166,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Assertion (A): Copper sulphate solution can be safely stored in a silver vessel.\nReason (R): The standard electrode potential of $Ag^+/Ag$ (0.80 V) is higher than that of $Cu^{2+}/Cu$ (0.34 V), so silver cannot reduce $Cu^{2+}$ ions.","image":"","options":["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"For Ag + $Cu^{2+}$, $E^\\circ_{cell}$ = 0.34 − 0.80 = −0.46 V, which is negative, so the reaction is non-spontaneous.\nSilver is therefore not attacked and A is true.\nR states the correct E° comparison, and this comparison is the reason A holds.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-167","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":167,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"For the Daniell cell (standard emf 1.1 V at 298 K), how many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) $E_{cell}$ increases if $[Cu^{2+}]$ is increased, keeping $[Zn^{2+}]$ fixed.\n(ii) If an opposing external voltage greater than 1.1 V is applied, zinc deposits at the zinc electrode and copper dissolves.\n(iii) When the cell reaches equilibrium, the voltmeter reads zero and $E^\\circ_{cell} = \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log K_c$.\n(iv) $K_c$ for the cell reaction is of the order of $10^{37}$.\n(v) $\\Delta_r G^\\circ$ for the reaction is about –212 kJ mol$^{-1}$.\n(vi) When the opposing external voltage equals exactly 1.1 V, current flows from Zn to Cu.\n(vii) Diluting the $ZnSO_4$ solution lowers $E_{cell}$.","image":"","options":["3","4","5","6"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: $E = E^\\circ - \\frac{0.059}{2}\\log\\frac{[Zn^{2+}]}{[Cu^{2+}]}$, so more $Cu^{2+}$ raises E.\n(ii) Correct: the cell now works as an electrolytic cell.\n(iii) Correct: at equilibrium, E = 0.\n(iv) Correct: $\\log K_c = 2.2/0.059 \\approx 37.3$, giving $K_c \\approx 2\\times10^{37}$.\n(v) Correct: $-2 \\times 96487 \\times 1.1 \\approx -212$ kJ.\n(vi) Incorrect: no current flows and no reaction occurs.\n(vii) Incorrect: lower $[Zn^{2+}]$ raises E.\nCount = 5.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-168","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":168,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I with List-II.\nList-I\n(P) Conductivity, $\\kappa$\n(Q) Molar conductivity, $\\Lambda_m$\n(R) Limiting molar conductivity, $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$\n(S) Constant A in $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - Ac^{1/2}$\nList-II\n(1) Molar conductivity as concentration approaches zero\n(2) Conductance of a unit volume of solution held between electrodes of unit area kept unit distance apart\n(3) Depends on the charges of the cation and anion, i.e., the type of electrolyte (1-1, 2-1, 2-2)\n(4) Conductance of the volume of solution containing one mole of electrolyte, held between electrodes kept unit distance apart","image":"","options":["P-2, Q-4, R-1, S-3","P-4, Q-2, R-1, S-3","P-2, Q-4, R-3, S-1","P-4, Q-2, R-3, S-1"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"P-2: $\\kappa$ is the conductance of one unit volume of solution, with A and l both unity.\nQ-4: $\\Lambda_m = \\kappa V$, where V is the volume containing 1 mol, with l = 1.\nR-1: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is the value of $\\Lambda_m$ as c tends to 0.\nS-3: for a given solvent and temperature, A depends on the ion charges, so all electrolytes of one type share the same A.\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-169","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":169,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) When an external opposing voltage greater than 1.1 V is applied to a Daniell cell, electrons flow from Cu to Zn and zinc is deposited on the zinc electrode.\n(ii) When the external opposing voltage equals 1.1 V, no current flows and no chemical reaction occurs.\n(iii) In rusting of iron, the overall reaction $2Fe + O_2 + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+} + 2H_2O$ has $E^\\circ_{cell} = 1.67$ V.\n(iv) Magnesium acts as a sacrificial electrode for iron because Mg has a higher standard reduction potential than Fe.\n(v) In a galvanic cell, the anode has a positive potential with respect to the solution.\n(vi) During discharge of a nickel-cadmium cell, Cd is converted into CdO.","image":"","options":["2","3","4","5"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: the cell then works as an electrolytic cell and the reaction is reversed.\n(ii) Correct: at 1.1 V the opposing voltage exactly balances the cell emf.\n(iii) Correct: $1.23 - (-0.44) = 1.67$ V.\n(iv) Incorrect: Mg ($-2.36$ V) has a lower reduction potential than Fe, so Mg corrodes preferentially.\n(v) Incorrect: the anode is negative with respect to the solution.\n(vi) Correct: $Cd + 2Ni(OH)_3 \\rightarrow CdO + 2Ni(OH)_2 + H_2O$.\nFour statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-170","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":170,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Three electrolytic cells are connected in series. They contain aqueous $AgNO_3$, aqueous $CuSO_4$, and molten $Al_2O_3$ in cryolite. A steady current is passed until 2.16 g of silver is deposited in the first cell. What is the total mass of metal deposited at all three cathodes? (Ag = 108, Cu = 63.5, Al = 27 g mol$^{-1}$)","image":"","options":["3.335 g","2.975 g","3.61 g","0.815 g"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: 2.16 g Ag, with $Ag^+ + e^- \\rightarrow Ag$.\nMoles of electrons = 2.16/108 = 0.02 mol. This is the same for every cell in series.\nCu: 0.02/2 = 0.01 mol, so mass = 0.01 × 63.5 = 0.635 g.\nAl: 0.02/3 mol, so mass = (0.02/3) × 27 = 0.18 g.\nTotal = 2.16 + 0.635 + 0.18 = 2.975 g.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-171","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":171,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"At 298 K, the limiting molar conductivities ($\\Lambda^\\circ_m$) of HCl, KCl and potassium propanoate ($CH_3CH_2COOK$) are 426.0, 150.0 and 109.6 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ respectively. A 0.0050 mol L$^{-1}$ solution of propanoic acid has conductivity $1.0 \\times 10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$. What is the dissociation constant of propanoic acid?","image":"","options":["$1.35\\times10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$1.42\\times10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$4.38\\times10^{-6}$ mol L$^{-1}$","$2.59\\times10^{-4}$ mol L$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa = 1.0\\times10^{-4}$ S cm$^{-1}$, c = 0.0050 mol L$^{-1}$.\nKohlrausch's law: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$(acid) = 426.0 + 109.6 – 150.0 = 385.6 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nMolar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m = \\frac{\\kappa \\times 1000}{c} = \\frac{1.0\\times10^{-4}\\times1000}{0.005} = 20$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nDegree of dissociation: $\\alpha = \\frac{20}{385.6} = 0.0519$.\nDissociation constant: $K_a = \\frac{c\\alpha^2}{1-\\alpha} = \\frac{0.005\\times0.0519^2}{0.948} = 1.42\\times10^{-5}$ mol L$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-172","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":172,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Using Kohlrausch law, calculate the limiting molar conductivity $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ of $Na_2SO_4$ at 298 K.\nGiven: $\\lambda^\\circ(Na^+) = 50.1$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(SO_4^{2-}) = 160.0$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.","image":"","options":["210.1 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","370.1 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","260.2 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","420.2 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"Given: $\\lambda^\\circ(Na^+) = 50.1$ and $\\lambda^\\circ(SO_4^{2-}) = 160.0$ S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nFormula: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = \\nu_+\\lambda^\\circ_+ + \\nu_-\\lambda^\\circ_-$. Here $Na_2SO_4$ gives $\\nu_+ = 2$ and $\\nu_- = 1$.\nSubstituting: $\\Lambda^\\circ_m = 2(50.1) + 160.0$.\nSo $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ = 260.2 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nOption (1) is wrong because it forgets to count two $Na^+$ ions.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-173","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":173,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A zinc half-cell ($Zn^{2+}$, 1 M) is connected to a standard hydrogen electrode through a salt bridge. $E^\\circ(Zn^{2+}/Zn)$ = −0.76 V. How many of the following statements are correct?\n(i) When the cell works spontaneously, the zinc electrode acts as the anode.\n(ii) In the external circuit, electrons flow from the platinum electrode to the zinc electrode.\n(iii) For the cell written as Pt|$H_2$|$H^+$||$Zn^{2+}$|Zn, E = −0.76 V, which shows that $H_2$ cannot reduce $Zn^{2+}$ under standard conditions.\n(iv) Hydrogen gas is evolved at the platinum electrode during spontaneous operation.\n(v) If the temperature is raised to 310 K, the SHE potential becomes non-zero.","image":"","options":["1","2","3","4"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["3"],"explanation":"(i) Correct: Zn has the lower E°, so it is oxidised and acts as the anode.\n(ii) Incorrect: electrons flow from the anode (Zn) to the cathode (Pt).\n(iii) Correct: a negative E for the written cell means the reaction as written is non-spontaneous.\n(iv) Correct: $H^+$ is reduced to $H_2$ at the Pt surface.\n(v) Incorrect: the SHE is assigned zero potential at all temperatures.\nThree statements are correct.\nAnswer: (3)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-174","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":174,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"The SI unit of electrical conductance, G, is the siemens (S). To which of the following is 1 S equal?","image":"","options":["$\\Omega$ m","$\\Omega^{-1}$","$\\Omega^{-1}$ m$^{-1}$","$\\Omega$ m$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Conductance is the reciprocal of resistance: $G = \\frac{1}{R}$.\nResistance is measured in ohm, so conductance is measured in ohm$^{-1}$. This unit is called siemens (S), also known as mho.\n$\\Omega^{-1}$ m$^{-1}$ (option 3) is tempting, but it is the unit of conductivity $\\kappa$, not of conductance.\n$\\Omega$ m is the unit of resistivity.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-175","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":175,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Match List-I (stage of rusting) with List-II (reaction).\nList-I:\n(a) Reaction at anodic spot\n(b) Reaction at cathodic spot\n(c) Atmospheric oxidation\n(d) Overall cell reaction\nList-II:\n(i) $2Fe^{2+} + 2H_2O + \\frac{1}{2}O_2 \\rightarrow Fe_2O_3 + 4H^+$\n(ii) $Fe \\rightarrow Fe^{2+} + 2e^-$\n(iii) $2Fe + O_2 + 4H^+ \\rightarrow 2Fe^{2+} + 2H_2O$\n(iv) $O_2 + 4H^+ + 4e^- \\rightarrow 2H_2O$","image":"","options":["(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["1"],"explanation":"(a) The anode is where oxidation of iron occurs, so it matches (ii).\n(b) The cathode is where $O_2$ is reduced in the presence of $H^+$, so it matches (iv).\n(c) In atmospheric oxidation, $Fe^{2+}$ is oxidised by air to $Fe_2O_3$, releasing $H^+$, so it matches (i).\n(d) Adding the anodic and cathodic reactions gives the overall cell reaction with $E^\\circ = 1.67$ V, so it matches (iii).\nAnswer: (1)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-176","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":176,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"A conductivity cell filled with 0.01 mol L$^{-1}$ KCl ($\\kappa$ = 0.141 S m$^{-1}$) shows a resistance of 200 $\\Omega$. The same cell filled with a 0.005 mol L$^{-1}$ solution of electrolyte X shows a resistance of 800 $\\Omega$. What is the molar conductivity of X?","image":"","options":["7.05 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","70.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","705 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$","35.25 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"Given: $\\kappa$(KCl) = 0.141 S m$^{-1}$, R(KCl) = 200 $\\Omega$, R(X) = 800 $\\Omega$, c(X) = 0.005 mol L$^{-1}$.\nCell constant: $G^*$ = 0.141 × 200 = 28.2 m$^{-1}$.\nConductivity of X: $\\kappa$ = 28.2/800 = 0.03525 S m$^{-1}$.\nConcentration in SI units: c = 0.005 × 1000 = 5 mol m$^{-3}$.\nMolar conductivity: $\\Lambda_m$ = 0.03525/5 = 7.05 × $10^{-3}$ S m$^2$ mol$^{-1}$ = 70.5 S cm$^2$ mol$^{-1}$.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"NCERT12-CHEM-ELECTROCHEMISTRY-177","exam":"JEE Main","year":null,"paper":"Practice · Electrochemistry","qno":177,"subject":"Chemistry","section":"","part":"","chapter":"Electrochemistry","type":"mcq","question":"Which of the following statements is INCORRECT?","image":"","options":["$E_{cell}$ is an intensive property, whereas $\\Delta_r G$ for the cell reaction depends on how the balanced equation is written","For a strong electrolyte, $\\Lambda_m$ decreases linearly with concentration $c$, so a plot of $\\Lambda_m$ against $c$ is a straight line with intercept $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$","NaCl and KBr have the same value of the constant 'A' in $\\Lambda_m = \\Lambda^\\circ_m - Ac^{1/2}$ in water at a given temperature","$\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ of acetic acid cannot be obtained reliably by extrapolating $\\Lambda_m$ to zero concentration, because $\\Lambda_m$ rises steeply at very low concentrations"],"optionsInImage":false,"answer":["2"],"explanation":"(1) Correct: doubling the equation changes $\\Delta_r G$ from $-2FE$ to $-4FE$, while $E_{cell}$ stays the same.\n(2) Incorrect: for strong electrolytes, $\\Lambda_m$ is linear in $c^{1/2}$, not in $c$.\n(3) Correct: both are 1-1 electrolytes, and all electrolytes of the same type have the same 'A'.\n(4) Correct: for weak electrolytes, $\\Lambda_m$ rises steeply near zero concentration, so $\\Lambda^\\circ_m$ is obtained from Kohlrausch's law instead.\nAnswer: (2)","source":"practice-ncert","credit":"SONEXA original (NCERT-based)","basis":"NCERT Class 12 Chemistry, Ch 2 · Electrochemistry","difficulty":"hard"}]});
